HEAT:9906195.statement:
Saturated water at p = 1.5 bar (Tsat = 111.4 °C, ρ_l = 950.7 kg/m³, ρ_v = 0.862 kg/m³, h_fg = 2226 kJ/kg, σ = 0.0568 N/m) is boiled on a large horizontal stainless-steel plate. The heater can deliver a uniform flux up to 1.80 MW/m². Using Zuber’s critical-heat-flux expression
q_max'' = (π/24) h_fg ρ_v [σ g (ρ_l − ρ_v) / ρ_v² ]^{1/4}
and the Lienhard–Dhir recommendation that q_max'' be multiplied by 1.14 for an infinite horizontal plate, determine q_max''. Compute the ratio of the heater’s available flux to this q_max'' and state whether a burnout (departure from nucleate boiling) is expected if the controller is set to 1.80 MW/m². If the same plate instead boils saturated R-134a at 4 °C (ρ_l = 1278 kg/m³, ρ_v = 16.0 kg/m³, h_fg = 191 kJ/kg, σ = 0.0110 N/m), recompute q_max'' with the same Lienhard–Dhir factor and determine the maximum safe heater flux defined as 70% of q_max''. Interpret, in one sentence, why the two fluids have different burnout margins at the same heater setting.

HEAT:9906239.canonicalSolution:
\(C_{\min}=C_{\mathrm{hot}}=4200\,\mathrm{W/K}\), \(C_r=2/3\), required \(\varepsilon=(160-75)/(160-35)=0.680\). Counterflow inversion \(\mathrm{NTU}=(1-C_r)^{-1}\ln[(1-C_r\varepsilon)/(1-\varepsilon)]=1.607\), \(A=\mathrm{NTU}C_{\min}/U=17.76\,\mathrm{m^2}\). Holding that NTU:
- CF: \(\varepsilon=0.680\), meets \(T_{h,o}=75^\circ\mathrm{C}\);
- unmixed crossflow: \(\varepsilon=0.632\);
- 1–2: \(\varepsilon=0.605\);
- PF: \(\varepsilon=0.548\).

Ranking by heat rate: CF > unmixed crossflow > 1–2 > PF. Only CF meets \(T_{h,o}=75^\circ\mathrm{C}\).

### Final answer

\(C_{\min}=4200\,\mathrm{W/K}\), \(C_r=2/3\), \(\varepsilon=0.680\), \(\mathrm{NTU}=1.607\), \(A=17.76\,\mathrm{m^2}\); ranking CF > unmixed crossflow > 1–2 > PF; only CF meets \(T_{h,o}=75^\circ\mathrm{C}\).

