THERMO:9907201.statement:
Moist air at \(101.0\,\mathrm{kPa}\) has a dry-bulb temperature of \(30.0^\circ\mathrm{C}\) and \(40\%\) relative humidity. The thermodynamic wet-bulb temperature is the adiabatic-saturation temperature \(T_2\) at which the energy balance \(h_1+(\omega_2-\omega_1)h_{f2}=h_2\) holds, with state 2 saturated at \(T_2\) and the same total pressure. Use \(\omega=0.622 p_v/(P-p_v)\) and \(h=1.005 T+\omega h_g(T)\) with \(T\) in °C. Inlet saturation data: \(p_g(30.0^\circ\mathrm{C})=4.247\,\mathrm{kPa}\), \(h_g(30.0^\circ\mathrm{C})=2556.3\,\mathrm{kJ/kg}\). Evaluate the residual \(r(T_2)=h_1+(\omega_{\mathrm{sat}}(T_2)-\omega_1)h_f(T_2)-h_{\mathrm{sat}}(T_2)\) at \(T_2=18.0\), \(20.0\), and \(22.0^\circ\mathrm{C}\) using
\(p_g(18^\circ\mathrm{C})=2.064\,\mathrm{kPa}\), \(h_g(18^\circ\mathrm{C})=2534.4\,\mathrm{kJ/kg}\), \(h_f(18^\circ\mathrm{C})=75.58\,\mathrm{kJ/kg}\);
\(p_g(20^\circ\mathrm{C})=2.339\,\mathrm{kPa}\), \(h_g(20^\circ\mathrm{C})=2538.1\,\mathrm{kJ/kg}\), \(h_f(20^\circ\mathrm{C})=83.96\,\mathrm{kJ/kg}\);
\(p_g(22^\circ\mathrm{C})=2.645\,\mathrm{kPa}\), \(h_g(22^\circ\mathrm{C})=2541.7\,\mathrm{kJ/kg}\), \(h_f(22^\circ\mathrm{C})=92.33\,\mathrm{kJ/kg}\).
Determine \(\omega_1\) and \(h_1\), the three residuals, and the wet-bulb temperature obtained by linear interpolation between the two neighbouring temperatures that bracket \(r=0\).

THERMO:9907261.statement:
A regenerative Rankine cycle with two open feedwater heaters is to have its extraction pressures chosen from a discrete admissible set. Boiler exit is fixed at \(10.0\,\mathrm{MPa}\), \(560^\circ\mathrm{C}\). Condenser is at \(8.00\,\mathrm{kPa}\). Turbine isentropic efficiency is \(0.86\) (one continuous expansion line; extractions are bled from the actual expansion). Pump isentropic efficiency is \(0.78\). Use only this property set; interpolate linearly in \(\ln P\) for superheated enthalpy and entropy at \(560^\circ\mathrm{C}\) and for isentropic-exit enthalpies, and use the saturation table directly at listed pressures.

Saturation (\(P\) in MPa, \(h\) in kJ/kg, \(s\) in kJ/kg·K, \(v_f\) in \(10^{-3}\,\mathrm{m^3/kg}\)):
\(P = 0.008\): \(h_f = 173.9\), \(h_g = 2573.6\), \(s_f = 0.592\), \(s_g = 8.229\), \(v_f = 1.008\);
\(P = 0.050\): \(h_f = 340.5\), \(h_g = 2645.9\), \(s_f = 1.091\), \(s_g = 7.594\), \(v_f = 1.030\);
\(P = 0.100\): \(h_f = 417.5\), \(h_g = 2675.5\), \(s_f = 1.303\), \(s_g = 7.359\), \(v_f = 1.043\);
\(P = 0.200\): \(h_f = 504.7\), \(h_g = 2706.7\), \(s_f = 1.530\), \(s_g = 7.127\), \(v_f = 1.061\);
\(P = 0.500\): \(h_f = 640.2\), \(h_g = 2748.7\), \(s_f = 1.861\), \(s_g = 6.821\), \(v_f = 1.093\);
\(P = 1.00\): \(h_f = 762.8\), \(h_g = 2778.1\), \(s_f = 2.138\), \(s_g = 6.586\), \(v_f = 1.127\);
\(P = 2.00\): \(h_f = 908.8\), \(h_g = 2799.5\), \(s_f = 2.447\), \(s_g = 6.340\), \(v_f = 1.177\);
\(P = 4.00\): \(h_f = 1087.3\), \(h_g = 2801.4\), \(s_f = 2.796\), \(s_g = 6.070\), \(v_f = 1.252\).

Superheat at \(10.0\,\mathrm{MPa}\), \(560^\circ\mathrm{C}\): \(h = 3511.0\), \(s = 6.756\). For an isentropic expansion from that state, \(h_s(P) = 3511.0 - 560.0 \ln(10.0/P) - 42.0 (P/10.0 - 1)\) with \(P\) in MPa and \(h_s\) in kJ/kg; if the resulting \(h_s\) lies below \(h_f + x_\mathrm{min} (h_g - h_f)\) at that \(P\), wet-state properties at \(s = 6.756\,\mathrm{kJ/kg\cdot K}\) must be used instead (\(x = (s - s_f)/(s_g - s_f)\), \(h = h_f + x h_{fg}\)). Actual extraction enthalpy is \(h = h_1 - 0.86(h_1 - h_s)\). Admissible extraction pairs \((P_\mathrm{high}, P_\mathrm{low})\) in MPa are \((4.00, 1.00)\), \((4.00, 0.50)\), \((4.00, 0.20)\), \((2.00, 0.50)\), \((2.00, 0.20)\), \((2.00, 0.10)\), \((1.00, 0.20)\), \((1.00, 0.10)\), \((1.00, 0.050)\). Each open heater produces saturated liquid at its own pressure. Steam flow at the boiler exit is \(35.0\,\mathrm{kg/s}\). Maximize net electrical power subject to turbine-exit quality \(x_6 \ge 0.88\). Report the optimal pair, \(y_\mathrm{high}\), \(y_\mathrm{low}\), \(\dot W_\mathrm{net}\), \(\eta_I\), \(\eta_{II}\) versus a \(1600\,\mathrm{K}\) source and \(T_0 = 298.15\,\mathrm{K}\), condenser duty, and \(\dot\sigma_\mathrm{universe}\). Rank the feasible pairs by \(\dot W_\mathrm{net}\) and by \(\eta_{II}\) and state whether the rankings coincide. Confirm energy closure of both heaters.

THERMO:9907262.statement:
A flashing two-phase receiver A of fixed volume \(0.40\,\mathrm{m^3}\) initially contains saturated water at \(180^\circ\mathrm{C}\) with liquid occupying \(80.0\) percent of the volume. Saturated vapour is withdrawn through an adiabatic turbine and discharged into receiver B, a constant-pressure condensing receiver maintained at \(40.0^\circ\mathrm{C}\) by a coolant. Receiver A is adiabatic; its contents remain saturated while mass leaves as saturated vapour at the instantaneous A state. The turbine isentropic efficiency is \(0.80\) based on an isentropic expansion from the instantaneous saturated-vapour state in A to \(P_\mathrm{sat}(40.0^\circ\mathrm{C})\). The mass-flow rate is \(\dot m=2.20\times 10^{-5}(P_A-P_B)\,\mathrm{kg/s}\) with pressures in pascals. Coolant keeps B at saturated \(40.0^\circ\mathrm{C}\); incoming vapour is condensed to saturated liquid, which accumulates in B. Use only this saturation table (\(T\) in \(^\circ\mathrm{C}\), \(P\) in kPa, \(v\) in m\(^3\)/kg, \(u\) and \(h\) in kJ/kg, \(s\) in kJ/kg·K) and interpolate linearly in \(T\):

\(T = 40\): \(P = 7.38\), \(v_f = 0.001008\), \(v_g = 19.52\), \(u_f = 167.5\), \(u_g = 2430.0\), \(h_f = 167.5\), \(h_g = 2574.9\), \(s_f = 0.572\), \(s_g = 8.257\);
\(T = 80\): \(P = 47.39\), \(v_f = 0.001029\), \(v_g = 3.407\), \(u_f = 334.9\), \(u_g = 2482.2\), \(h_f = 334.9\), \(h_g = 2643.8\), \(s_f = 1.075\), \(s_g = 7.612\);
\(T = 120\): \(P = 198.5\), \(v_f = 0.001060\), \(v_g = 0.8919\), \(u_f = 503.5\), \(u_g = 2529.5\), \(h_f = 503.7\), \(h_g = 2706.3\), \(s_f = 1.528\), \(s_g = 7.130\);
\(T = 140\): \(P = 361.3\), \(v_f = 0.001080\), \(v_g = 0.5085\), \(u_f = 588.7\), \(u_g = 2549.9\), \(h_f = 589.1\), \(h_g = 2733.7\), \(s_f = 1.739\), \(s_g = 6.929\);
\(T = 160\): \(P = 618.1\), \(v_f = 0.001102\), \(v_g = 0.3066\), \(u_f = 674.9\), \(u_g = 2567.8\), \(h_f = 675.5\), \(h_g = 2758.1\), \(s_f = 1.942\), \(s_g = 6.746\);
\(T = 180\): \(P = 1002.7\), \(v_f = 0.001127\), \(v_g = 0.1940\), \(u_f = 762.0\), \(u_g = 2583.7\), \(h_f = 763.2\), \(h_g = 2778.2\), \(s_f = 2.139\), \(s_g = 6.584\).

Integrate the coupled mass and energy balances on A, with \(V_f + V_g = 0.40\,\mathrm{m^3}\), until \(T_A\) first reaches \(120^\circ\mathrm{C}\). Report the time, mass of vapour withdrawn, turbine work, liquid volume remaining in A, coolant duty in B, entropy production in A, in the turbine, in B, and in the universe (coolant at \(40.0^\circ\mathrm{C}\)). Confirm \(\Delta U_A + \Delta U_B + W_t + Q_\mathrm{coolant} = 0\) (\(Q_\mathrm{coolant}\) is heat leaving B) and \(I_\mathrm{total} = T_0\sigma_\mathrm{universe}\) with \(T_0 = 313.15\,\mathrm{K}\).

CIR:9908031.statement:
A connected resistive network has three essential nodes labelled \(A\), \(B\), and \(N\). Take \(N\) as the reference (ground) node, and let \(v_A\) and \(v_B\) be the voltages of \(A\) and \(B\) with respect to \(N\). The branches are as follows:
- a \(2\,\Omega\) resistor between \(A\) and \(N\);
- a \(4\,\Omega\) resistor between \(A\) and \(B\);
- a \(4\,\Omega\) resistor between \(B\) and \(N\);
- an independent current source of \(3\,\mathrm{A}\) connected between \(N\) and \(A\), with its reference arrow directed from \(N\) toward \(A\);
- an independent current source of \(1\,\mathrm{A}\) connected between \(N\) and \(B\), with its reference arrow directed from \(N\) toward \(B\).

Using systematic nodal analysis, write the two independent KCL equations in conductance matrix form \(G\begin{bmatrix}v_A\\v_B\end{bmatrix}=i\). State every entry of \(G\) in siemens and every entry of \(i\) in amperes, with the unknown vector ordered as \((v_A,v_B)\). Solve for \(v_A\) and \(v_B\) in volts. Then compute the current through the \(4\,\Omega\) resistor between \(A\) and \(B\), taken positive from \(A\) toward \(B\).

CIR:9908032.statement:
A ladder network has essential nodes \(A\), \(B\), \(C\), and reference node \(N\). Node voltages \(v_A\), \(v_B\), and \(v_C\) are measured with respect to \(N\). The branches are:
- \(2\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(N\);
- \(4\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(B\);
- \(8\,\Omega\) between \(B\) and \(N\);
- \(8\,\Omega\) between \(B\) and \(C\);
- \(8\,\Omega\) between \(C\) and \(N\);
- an independent \(3\,\mathrm{A}\) source between \(N\) and \(A\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(A\);
- an independent \(1\,\mathrm{A}\) source between \(N\) and \(C\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(C\).

Assemble the \(3\times 3\) nodal conductance matrix \(G\) and the injected-current vector \(i\) for the unknown vector \((v_A,v_B,v_C)\). Write \(Gv=i\) with every numerical entry and its unit. Solve the linear system for \(v_A\), \(v_B\), and \(v_C\) in volts.

CIR:9908033.statement:
A network has essential nodes \(A\), \(B\), \(C\), and reference node \(N\). An independent voltage source of \(12\,\mathrm{V}\) is connected between \(A\) and \(N\), with its positive terminal at \(A\) and its negative terminal at \(N\), so that \(v_A\) is known. The remaining branches are:
- \(4\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(B\);
- \(6\,\Omega\) between \(B\) and \(N\);
- \(3\,\Omega\) between \(B\) and \(C\);
- \(6\,\Omega\) between \(C\) and \(N\);
- an independent \(2\,\mathrm{A}\) current source between \(N\) and \(C\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(C\).

Write the independent nodal equations at the unknown-voltage nodes \(B\) and \(C\) only. Solve for \(v_B\) and \(v_C\) in volts. Then determine the current in the \(4\,\Omega\) resistor, positive from \(A\) toward \(B\), and the current in the voltage source, positive from \(A\) through the source toward \(N\).

CIR:9908034.statement:
A network has essential nodes \(A\), \(B\), and reference node \(N\). A floating independent voltage source of \(8\,\mathrm{V}\) is connected between \(A\) and \(B\), with its positive terminal at \(A\) and its negative terminal at \(B\). The remaining branches are:
- \(2\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(N\);
- \(4\,\Omega\) between \(B\) and \(N\);
- an independent \(3\,\mathrm{A}\) current source between \(N\) and \(A\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(A\);
- an independent \(0.5\,\mathrm{A}\) current source between \(N\) and \(B\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(B\).

Identify the supernode associated with the floating voltage source. Write the supernode KCL equation, using currents leaving the supernode through the two resistors as positive, together with the voltage-source constraint relating \(v_A\) and \(v_B\). Solve for \(v_A\) and \(v_B\) in volts. Then find the current through the \(8\,\mathrm{V}\) source, taken positive from \(A\) through the source toward \(B\).

CIR:9908035.statement:
A network has essential nodes \(A\), \(B\), \(C\), and reference node \(N\). A floating independent voltage source of \(6\,\mathrm{V}\) has its positive terminal at \(A\) and its negative terminal at \(B\). The remaining branches are:
- \(4\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(N\);
- \(5\,\Omega\) between \(B\) and \(N\);
- \(8\,\Omega\) between \(B\) and \(C\);
- \(2\,\Omega\) between \(C\) and \(N\);
- an independent \(1\,\mathrm{A}\) current source between \(N\) and \(A\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(A\);
- an independent \(2\,\mathrm{A}\) current source between \(N\) and \(C\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(C\).

Form a supernode enclosing \(A\) and \(B\). Write the supernode KCL equation, the ordinary nodal equation at \(C\), and the voltage constraint. Solve for \(v_A\), \(v_B\), and \(v_C\) in volts. Report the current in the \(8\,\Omega\) resistor, positive from \(B\) toward \(C\).

CIR:9908037.statement:
A network has essential nodes \(A\), \(B\), \(C\), and reference node \(N\). The branches are:
- an independent \(2\,\mathrm{A}\) current source between \(N\) and \(A\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(A\);
- \(4\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(N\);
- \(8\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(B\);
- \(12\,\Omega\) between \(B\) and \(N\);
- \(6\,\Omega\) between \(C\) and \(N\);
- a voltage-controlled voltage source connected between \(B\) and \(C\), with its negative terminal at \(B\), its positive terminal at \(C\), and value \(2v_A\), so that \(v_C-v_B=2v_A\).

Identify the supernode enclosing \(B\) and \(C\). Write KCL at \(A\), supernode KCL at \(\{B,C\}\), and the dependent-source constraint. Solve for \(v_A\), \(v_B\), and \(v_C\) in volts. Then find the current through the dependent voltage source, taken positive from \(C\) through the source toward \(B\).

CIR:9908040.statement:
A network has essential nodes \(A\), \(B\), \(C\), and reference node \(N\). An independent voltage source of \(10\,\mathrm{V}\) is connected between \(A\) and \(N\), positive terminal at \(A\), so \(v_A=10\,\mathrm{V}\) is known. The remaining branches are:
- \(2\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(B\);
- \(4\,\Omega\) between \(B\) and \(N\);
- \(4\,\Omega\) between \(B\) and \(C\);
- \(8\,\Omega\) between \(C\) and \(N\);
- \(8\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(C\);
- an independent \(1\,\mathrm{A}\) current source between \(N\) and \(B\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(B\);
- an independent \(0.5\,\mathrm{A}\) current source between \(N\) and \(C\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(C\).

First write the unreduced \(3\times 3\) conductance stamp that would apply to \((v_A,v_B,v_C)\) if node \(A\) were unknown, including the injected-current vector from the two current sources only. Then reduce the system by substituting \(v_A=10\,\mathrm{V}\), obtaining a \(2\times 2\) matrix equation for \((v_B,v_C)\). Solve for \(v_B\) and \(v_C\) in volts.

CIR:9908041.statement:
A network has essential nodes \(A\), \(B\), and reference node \(N\). A floating independent voltage source of \(10\,\mathrm{V}\) has its positive terminal at \(A\) and its negative terminal at \(B\). In parallel with that voltage source, an independent current source of \(2\,\mathrm{A}\) is connected between \(A\) and \(B\), with its reference arrow directed from \(A\) toward \(B\). The remaining branches are:
- \(4\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(N\);
- \(5\,\Omega\) between \(B\) and \(N\);
- an independent \(1\,\mathrm{A}\) current source between \(N\) and \(A\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(A\);
- an independent \(0.5\,\mathrm{A}\) current source between \(N\) and \(B\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(B\).

Form the supernode enclosing \(A\) and \(B\). Write supernode KCL for currents leaving the supernode through the two resistors to \(N\), and explain whether the \(2\,\mathrm{A}\) source appears in that supernode equation. Write the voltage constraint. Solve for \(v_A\) and \(v_B\) in volts. Then, using KCL at node \(A\) alone, determine the current through the \(10\,\mathrm{V}\) source, positive from \(A\) through the voltage source toward \(B\).

CIR:9908042.statement:
A network has essential nodes \(A\), \(B\), \(C\), and reference node \(N\). Six resistors and two current sources are connected as follows:
- \(4\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(N\);
- \(4\,\Omega\) between \(B\) and \(N\);
- \(2\,\Omega\) between \(C\) and \(N\);
- \(4\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(B\);
- \(4\,\Omega\) between \(B\) and \(C\);
- \(4\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(C\);
- an independent \(3\,\mathrm{A}\) source between \(N\) and \(A\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(A\);
- an independent \(1\,\mathrm{A}\) source between \(N\) and \(B\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(B\).

By the inspection rules for nodal conductance form, write the matrix equation \(G\begin{bmatrix}v_A\\v_B\\v_C\end{bmatrix}=i\). State the rule used for each diagonal entry, each off-diagonal entry, and each entry of \(i\). Solve for the three node voltages in volts.

CIR:9908043.statement:
A network has essential nodes \(A\), \(B\), and reference node \(N\). The branches are:
- \(5\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(N\);
- \(10\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(B\);
- \(10\,\Omega\) between \(B\) and \(N\);
- an independent \(2\,\mathrm{A}\) current source between \(N\) and \(A\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(A\);
- a voltage-controlled current source connected between \(B\) and \(N\), with its reference arrow directed from \(B\) toward \(N\) and with value \(0.2v_A\) amperes, where \(v_A\) is in volts.

Write KCL at \(A\) and at \(B\) with currents leaving each node taken as positive. Arrange the equations as \(G\begin{bmatrix}v_A\\v_B\end{bmatrix}=i\) and evaluate every entry of \(G\) in siemens. State whether \(G\) is symmetric, and identify which entry is produced by the dependent source. Solve for \(v_A\) and \(v_B\) in volts.

CIR:9908044.statement:
A network has essential nodes \(A\), \(B\), and reference node \(N\). The branches are:
- \(4\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(N\);
- \(6\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(B\);
- \(3\,\Omega\) between \(B\) and \(N\);
- an independent \(2\,\mathrm{A}\) current source between \(N\) and \(A\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(A\);
- an independent \(1\,\mathrm{A}\) current source between \(B\) and \(N\), arrow from \(B\) toward \(N\).

Write the two nodal equations in conductance form and solve for \(v_A\) and \(v_B\) in volts. Using those voltages, compute every resistor current with the passive-sign orientation (current entering the terminal at higher potential when the resistor voltage is positive). Then write KCL at \(A\), at \(B\), and at the reference node \(N\), taking currents leaving each node as positive, and verify that all three balances close. State why the reference-node KCL is not an independent equation.

CIR:9908045.statement:
A network has essential nodes \(A\), \(B\), \(C\), and reference node \(N\). Two floating independent voltage sources share node \(B\): a \(6\,\mathrm{V}\) source has its positive terminal at \(A\) and its negative terminal at \(B\), and a \(4\,\mathrm{V}\) source has its positive terminal at \(C\) and its negative terminal at \(B\). The remaining branches are:
- \(3\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(N\);
- \(6\,\Omega\) between \(C\) and \(N\);
- an independent \(2\,\mathrm{A}\) current source between \(N\) and \(B\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(B\).

There is no resistor connected directly from \(B\) to \(N\). Identify a single supernode enclosing \(A\), \(B\), and \(C\). Write supernode KCL and the two voltage constraints. Solve for \(v_A\), \(v_B\), and \(v_C\) in volts. Then find the current in the \(6\,\mathrm{V}\) source, positive from \(A\) through that source toward \(B\), and the current in the \(4\,\mathrm{V}\) source, positive from \(C\) through that source toward \(B\).

CIR:9908048.statement:
A network has essential nodes \(A\), \(B\), \(C\), and reference node \(N\). Branch elements are given in mixed resistance and conductance form:
- a conductance of \(0.50\,\mathrm{S}\) between \(A\) and \(N\);
- a conductance of \(0.25\,\mathrm{S}\) between \(A\) and \(B\);
- a conductance of \(0.25\,\mathrm{S}\) between \(B\) and \(N\);
- a conductance of \(0.20\,\mathrm{S}\) between \(B\) and \(C\);
- a conductance of \(0.30\,\mathrm{S}\) between \(C\) and \(N\);
- an independent \(4\,\mathrm{A}\) current source between \(N\) and \(A\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(A\);
- an independent \(1\,\mathrm{A}\) current source between \(C\) and \(N\), arrow from \(C\) toward \(N\).

Write the nodal system in conductance form \(Gv=i\) for \(v=(v_A,v_B,v_C)\), with \(G\) in siemens and \(i\) in amperes. Solve for the three node voltages in volts. Substitute the solution back into \(Gv-i\) and show that the residual vector is the zero vector, thereby verifying the matrix equation.

CIR:9908049.statement:
A network has essential nodes \(A\), \(B\), and reference node \(N\). A floating independent voltage source of \(6\,\mathrm{V}\) has its positive terminal at \(A\) and its negative terminal at \(B\). The remaining branches are:
- \(3\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(N\);
- \(6\,\Omega\) between \(B\) and \(N\);
- an independent \(2\,\mathrm{A}\) current source between \(N\) and \(A\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(A\);
- a voltage-controlled current source connected between \(N\) and \(B\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(B\), with value \(0.25v_A\) amperes (\(v_A\) in volts).

Form the supernode enclosing \(A\) and \(B\). Write supernode KCL, counting both independent and dependent current sources that attach the supernode to \(N\), together with the voltage constraint. Solve for \(v_A\) and \(v_B\) in volts. Then find the current through the \(6\,\mathrm{V}\) source, positive from \(A\) through the source toward \(B\).

CIR:9908050.statement:
A network has essential nodes \(A\), \(B\), \(C\), and reference node \(N\). An independent voltage source of \(12\,\mathrm{V}\) is connected between \(A\) and \(N\), positive terminal at \(A\). A second independent voltage source of \(5\,\mathrm{V}\) is floating between \(B\) and \(C\), with its positive terminal at \(B\) and its negative terminal at \(C\). The remaining branches are:
- \(4\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(B\);
- \(6\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(C\);
- \(3\,\Omega\) between \(C\) and \(N\);
- an independent \(1\,\mathrm{A}\) current source between \(N\) and \(B\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(B\).

Using \(v_A=12\,\mathrm{V}\) and a supernode enclosing \(B\) and \(C\), write the independent KCL equation and the floating-source constraint. Solve for \(v_B\) and \(v_C\) in volts. Then determine the current in the \(4\,\Omega\) resistor, positive from \(A\) toward \(B\), and the current in the \(5\,\mathrm{V}\) source, positive from \(B\) through that source toward \(C\).

CIR:9908051.statement:
A network has essential nodes \(A\), \(B\), \(C\), and reference node \(N\). The branches are:
- \(4\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(N\);
- \(2\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(B\);
- \(4\,\Omega\) between \(B\) and \(N\);
- \(4\,\Omega\) between \(B\) and \(C\);
- \(4\,\Omega\) between \(C\) and \(N\);
- \(4\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(C\);
- an independent \(2\,\mathrm{A}\) current source between \(N\) and \(A\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(A\);
- an independent \(2\,\mathrm{A}\) current source between \(N\) and \(C\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(C\).

Write the \(3\times 3\) nodal conductance equation for \((v_A,v_B,v_C)\). Solve the system for the three node voltages in volts. Then compute the three currents leaving node \(B\) through the resistors incident on \(B\), and verify that their algebraic sum is zero.

CIR:9908053.statement:
A network has essential nodes \(A\), \(B\), and reference node \(N\). The branches are:
- \(4\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(N\);
- \(2\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(B\);
- \(8\,\Omega\) between \(B\) and \(N\);
- an independent \(2\,\mathrm{A}\) current source between \(N\) and \(A\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(A\);
- a voltage-controlled current source connected between \(B\) and \(N\), arrow from \(B\) toward \(N\), with value \(0.25v_A\) amperes;
- a second voltage-controlled current source connected between \(N\) and \(A\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(A\), with value \(0.25v_B\) amperes.

Write KCL at \(A\) and at \(B\) with currents leaving each node taken as positive. Cast the system as \(G\begin{bmatrix}v_A\\v_B\end{bmatrix}=i\) and report every entry of \(G\) in siemens and of \(i\) in amperes. Solve for \(v_A\) and \(v_B\) in volts. State which contributions prevent \(G\) from equalling the ordinary resistor stamp of the three conductances alone.

CIR:9908054.statement:
A network has essential nodes \(A\), \(B\), and reference node \(N\). The branches are:
- an independent \(12\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source between \(A\) and \(N\), positive terminal at \(A\);
- \(6\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(B\);
- \(3\,\Omega\) between \(B\) and \(N\);
- an independent \(2\,\mathrm{A}\) current source between \(N\) and \(B\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(B\).

Using nodal analysis, determine \(v_B\) in volts and the current \(i_S\) in the voltage source, positive from \(A\) through the source toward \(N\). For every element, compute absorbed power under the passive-sign convention: \(p=vi\) only when the referenced current enters the referenced positive-voltage terminal. Tabulate absorbed power for the \(6\,\Omega\) resistor, the \(3\,\Omega\) resistor, the voltage source, and the current source, in watts. Verify that the four absorbed powers sum to zero, and identify which elements deliver power in this operating point.

CIR:9908055.statement:
A network has essential nodes \(A\), \(B\), and reference node \(N\). The branches are:
- \(4\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(N\);
- \(6\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(B\);
- \(3\,\Omega\) between \(B\) and \(N\);
- an independent \(2\,\mathrm{A}\) current source between \(N\) and \(A\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(A\);
- an independent \(1\,\mathrm{A}\) current source between \(N\) and \(B\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(B\).

A proposed operating point is \(v_A=6\,\mathrm{V}\), \(v_B=3\,\mathrm{V}\). Write the two nodal KCL residuals (currents leaving each nonreference node, minus the injected source currents) at this proposed point, in amperes. Decide whether the proposal satisfies both nodal equations. If it does not, solve the conductance system for the correct \(v_A\) and \(v_B\) in volts, and recompute both residuals at the corrected solution.

CIR:9908057.statement:
A connected resistive network has three essential nodes \(P\), \(Q\), and \(R\). The branches are:
- \(2\,\Omega\) between \(P\) and \(Q\);
- \(4\,\Omega\) between \(Q\) and \(R\);
- \(4\,\Omega\) between \(P\) and \(R\);
- an independent \(3\,\mathrm{A}\) current source between \(R\) and \(P\), arrow from \(R\) toward \(P\);
- an independent \(1\,\mathrm{A}\) current source between \(Q\) and \(R\), arrow from \(Q\) toward \(R\).

First take \(R\) as the reference node and let \(v_P\) and \(v_Q\) be voltages with respect to \(R\). Write the conductance-form nodal equations and solve for \(v_P\) and \(v_Q\). Compute the three branch voltages \(v_{PQ}\), \(v_{QR}\), and \(v_{PR}\), each defined as the potential of the first-named node minus the potential of the second.

Next take \(P\) as the reference node instead, and let \(v'_Q\) and \(v'_R\) be voltages with respect to \(P\). Write and solve the new \(2\times 2\) nodal system. From \(v'_Q\) and \(v'_R\) recompute \(v_{PQ}\), \(v_{QR}\), and \(v_{PR}\). Verify that the three branch voltages are independent of the reference choice, and state the relation between the two sets of node potentials.

CIR:9908060.statement:
A network has essential nodes \(A\), \(B\), \(C\), and reference node \(N\). The branches are:
- a floating independent voltage source of \(12\,\mathrm{V}\) with positive terminal at \(A\) and negative terminal at \(B\);
- an independent \(3\,\mathrm{A}\) current source between \(N\) and \(A\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(A\);
- \(2\,\Omega\) between \(A\) and \(N\);
- \(4\,\Omega\) between \(B\) and \(N\);
- \(6\,\Omega\) between \(B\) and \(C\);
- \(3\,\Omega\) between \(C\) and \(N\);
- a voltage-controlled current source between \(N\) and \(C\), arrow from \(N\) toward \(C\), with value \(0.25v_C\) amperes (\(v_C\) in volts).

(a) Identify every essential node, the supernode, and the unknown node voltages. State how many independent KCL equations and how many voltage-source constraints are required.
(b) Write the supernode KCL equation for \(\{A,B\}\), the ordinary nodal equation at \(C\), and the voltage constraint. If you use matrix notation, display the equations in an equivalent mixed conductance-and-constraint form.
(c) Solve for \(v_A\), \(v_B\), and \(v_C\) in volts.
(d) Compute every resistor current with a declared direction, the current through the \(12\,\mathrm{V}\) source (positive from \(A\) toward \(B\) through the source), and the dependent-source current through its declared arrow. Verify KCL at \(A\), at \(B\), at \(C\), and at \(N\).
(e) Using the passive-sign convention, compute absorbed power in every element and verify that the algebraic sum of all absorbed powers is zero.

CIR:9908061.statement:
A connected planar resistive circuit is embedded as two adjacent rectangular windows that share a single vertical branch. The entire bottom rail is an ideal ground conductor. Label the top-left node \(A\), the top node of the shared branch \(B\), and the top-right node \(C\). The bottom terminals beneath \(A\), \(B\), and \(C\) are all ground.

The branches are as follows:
- an independent \(18\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from ground to \(A\), with the positive terminal at \(A\);
- a \(3\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(B\);
- a \(6\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to ground (the shared vertical branch);
- a \(4\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to \(C\);
- an independent \(6\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from ground to \(C\), with the positive terminal at \(C\).

Take clockwise mesh current \(I_1\) in the left window, circulating through the \(18\,\mathrm{V}\) source, the \(3\,\Omega\) resistor, and the \(6\,\Omega\) resistor. Take clockwise mesh current \(I_2\) in the right window, circulating through the \(6\,\Omega\) resistor, the \(4\,\Omega\) resistor, and the \(6\,\mathrm{V}\) source.

(a) Write the two independent mesh-current KVL equations, using the assigned clockwise senses.
(b) Solve for \(I_1\) and \(I_2\).
(c) Determine the net current directed downward through the \(6\,\Omega\) resistor.

CIR:9908062.statement:
A connected planar circuit occupies two adjacent rectangular windows sharing a vertical resistor. Label the corners of the left window \(A\) (top-left), \(B\) (top-right of the left window), \(E\) (bottom-right of the left window), and \(D\) (bottom-left). Label the remaining corners of the right window \(C\) (top-right) and \(F\) (bottom-right). Nodes \(D\), \(E\), and \(F\) are distinct; the bottom rail is not a short circuit.

The branches are as follows:
- an independent \(15\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from \(D\) to \(A\), with the positive terminal at \(A\);
- a \(2\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(B\);
- a \(5\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to \(E\) (shared vertical branch);
- a \(3\,\Omega\) resistor from \(E\) to \(D\);
- a \(6\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to \(C\);
- an independent \(10\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from \(F\) to \(C\), with the positive terminal at \(C\);
- a \(4\,\Omega\) resistor from \(F\) to \(E\).

Take clockwise mesh current \(I_1\) around \(A\)-\(B\)-\(E\)-\(D\) and clockwise mesh current \(I_2\) around \(B\)-\(C\)-\(F\)-\(E\).

(a) Write the two mesh-current KVL equations.
(b) Solve for \(I_1\) and \(I_2\).
(c) Taking node \(D\) as the voltage reference of \(0\,\mathrm{V}\), determine \(v_B\) and \(v_E\).

CIR:9908063.statement:
A connected planar ladder occupies three adjacent rectangular windows in a horizontal row. The entire bottom rail is ground. Label the successive top nodes, from left to right, \(A\), \(B\), \(C\), and \(D\). The bottom terminal of every vertical branch is ground.

The branches are as follows:
- an independent \(24\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from ground to \(A\), with the positive terminal at \(A\);
- a \(2\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(B\);
- a \(5\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to ground (shared by the left and middle windows);
- a \(3\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to \(C\);
- a \(6\,\Omega\) resistor from \(C\) to ground (shared by the middle and right windows);
- a \(4\,\Omega\) resistor from \(C\) to \(D\);
- an \(8\,\Omega\) resistor from \(D\) to ground.

Take clockwise mesh currents \(I_1\), \(I_2\), and \(I_3\) in the left, middle, and right windows respectively.

(a) Write the three independent mesh-current KVL equations.
(b) Solve for \(I_1\), \(I_2\), and \(I_3\).
(c) Determine the current directed from \(B\) to \(C\) through the \(3\,\Omega\) resistor.

CIR:9908064.statement:
A connected lumped network has exactly four nodes, labelled \(A\), \(B\), \(C\), and ground \(G\), and exactly six branches:
1. an independent \(12\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from \(G\) to \(A\), with the positive terminal at \(A\);
2. a \(2\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(B\);
3. a \(4\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to \(G\);
4. a \(6\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to \(C\);
5. a \(3\,\Omega\) resistor from \(C\) to \(G\);
6. a \(5\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(C\).

The network is planar. A planar embedding is used in which \(G\) lies on the unbounded outer face, branches 1--5 form a two-window ladder along the bottom rail \(G\), and branch 6 is drawn in the upper half-plane so that it does not cross any other branch.

(a) Count the number of nodes \(n\) and branches \(b\). Compute the dimension of the cycle space \(b-n+1\) and the number of bounded faces of the stated embedding. Confirm that these two integers agree.
(b) Identify a basis of three window meshes suitable for mesh-current analysis, naming the nodes of each window in clockwise order, and state which branch is shared by each adjacent pair of windows.
(c) Consider the additional closed walk \(G\)-\(A\)-\(B\)-\(C\)-\(G\) that uses branches 1, 2, 4, and 5. Explain, without solving the circuit, why the KVL statement on this walk is linearly dependent on the three window-mesh KVL statements, and why it must not be added as a fourth independent mesh equation.

CIR:9908066.statement:
A connected planar circuit occupies two adjacent rectangular windows whose shared vertical branch is an independent voltage source. The bottom rail is ground. Label the top-left node \(A\), the top node of the shared source \(B\), and the top-right node \(C\).

The branches are as follows:
- an independent \(10\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from ground to \(A\), with the positive terminal at \(A\) (the entire left vertical);
- a \(4\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(B\);
- an independent \(8\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from ground to \(B\), with the positive terminal at \(B\) (the entire shared vertical);
- a \(6\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to \(C\);
- a \(5\,\Omega\) resistor from \(C\) to ground.

Take clockwise mesh current \(I_1\) in the left window and clockwise mesh current \(I_2\) in the right window. The current through the shared \(8\,\mathrm{V}\) source, directed downward, is \(I_1-I_2\).

(a) Write the two mesh-current KVL equations, treating the shared source voltage as known.
(b) Solve for \(I_1\) and \(I_2\).
(c) Determine the current leaving the positive terminal of the \(8\,\mathrm{V}\) source.

CIR:9908067.statement:
A connected planar circuit is embedded as an L-shaped union of three rectangular windows: a north-west window, a north-east window sharing the north-west window’s right side, and a south-west window sharing the north-west window’s bottom side. There is no south-east window.

Label the nodes as follows: \(A\) north-west top-left, \(B\) top centre, \(C\) top-right, \(D\) mid-left, \(E\) centre, \(F\) mid-right, \(G\) bottom-left, and \(H\) bottom centre. There is no node at the missing south-east corner.

The branches are as follows:
- an independent \(20\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from \(D\) to \(A\), with the positive terminal at \(A\);
- a \(2\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(B\);
- a \(4\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to \(E\) (vertical shared by the two upper windows);
- a \(3\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to \(C\);
- a \(6\,\Omega\) resistor from \(C\) to \(F\);
- a \(5\,\Omega\) resistor from \(F\) to \(E\);
- a \(1\,\Omega\) resistor from \(D\) to \(E\) (horizontal shared by the two left windows);
- an \(8\,\Omega\) resistor from \(D\) to \(G\);
- a \(10\,\Omega\) resistor from \(G\) to \(H\);
- a \(7\,\Omega\) resistor from \(H\) to \(E\).

Take clockwise mesh current \(I_1\) around \(A\)-\(B\)-\(E\)-\(D\), clockwise \(I_2\) around \(B\)-\(C\)-\(F\)-\(E\), and clockwise \(I_3\) around \(D\)-\(E\)-\(H\)-\(G\).

(a) Write the three independent mesh-current KVL equations.
(b) Solve for \(I_1\), \(I_2\), and \(I_3\).
(c) Determine the current directed from \(D\) to \(E\) and the current directed from \(B\) to \(E\).

CIR:9908068.statement:
A connected planar circuit occupies two adjacent rectangular windows. The bottom rail is ground. Label the top-left node \(A\) and the top-right node \(B\).

The branches are as follows:
- an independent \(2\,\mathrm{A}\) current source occupying the entire left vertical branch, with its arrow directed upward from ground to \(A\);
- a \(5\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(B\);
- a \(4\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to ground;
- a \(3\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to ground (the shared vertical branch).

Take clockwise mesh current \(I_1\) in the left window and clockwise mesh current \(I_2\) in the right window.

(a) Explain why the left-hand mesh current is known immediately from the perimeter current source, and write the numerical constraint on \(I_1\), paying attention to the assigned clockwise sense.
(b) Write the single remaining independent mesh-current KVL equation, for the right window.
(c) Solve for \(I_2\) and determine the current directed downward through the \(3\,\Omega\) resistor.

CIR:9908069.statement:
A connected planar circuit occupies two adjacent rectangular windows whose shared vertical branch is an independent current source. The bottom rail is ground. Label the top-left node \(A\), the top node of the shared source \(B\), and the top-right node \(C\).

The branches are as follows:
- an independent \(12\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from ground to \(A\), with the positive terminal at \(A\);
- a \(2\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(B\);
- an independent \(2\,\mathrm{A}\) current source from \(B\) to ground, with its arrow directed downward (the entire shared vertical branch);
- a \(6\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to \(C\);
- a \(3\,\Omega\) resistor from \(C\) to ground.

Take clockwise mesh current \(I_1\) in the left window and clockwise mesh current \(I_2\) in the right window.

(a) Write the supermesh KVL equation obtained by traversing the outer closed walk of the two windows clockwise (through the \(12\,\mathrm{V}\) source, the \(2\,\Omega\) resistor, the \(6\,\Omega\) resistor, and the \(3\,\Omega\) resistor, thereby excluding the current source).
(b) Write the auxiliary constraint that the shared current source imposes on \(I_1\) and \(I_2\).
(c) Solve for \(I_1\) and \(I_2\), and determine the voltage \(v_B\) of node \(B\) with respect to ground, with the positive polarity at \(B\).

CIR:9908070.statement:
A connected planar ladder occupies three adjacent rectangular windows in a row. The bottom rail is ground. Label the top nodes, from left to right, \(A\), \(B\), \(C\), and \(D\).

The branches are as follows:
- an independent \(18\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from ground to \(A\), with the positive terminal at \(A\);
- a \(3\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(B\);
- an independent \(1\,\mathrm{A}\) current source from \(B\) to ground, with its arrow directed downward (shared by the left and middle windows);
- a \(4\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to \(C\);
- a \(5\,\Omega\) resistor from \(C\) to ground (shared by the middle and right windows);
- a \(2\,\Omega\) resistor from \(C\) to \(D\);
- a \(6\,\Omega\) resistor from \(D\) to ground.

Take clockwise mesh currents \(I_1\), \(I_2\), and \(I_3\) in the left, middle, and right windows respectively.

(a) Identify which pair of windows must be combined into a supermesh, and write that supermesh KVL equation together with the current-source constraint on \(I_1\) and \(I_2\).
(b) Write the ordinary mesh-current KVL equation for the right window.
(c) Solve for \(I_1\), \(I_2\), and \(I_3\).

CIR:9908071.statement:
A connected planar ladder occupies three adjacent rectangular windows. The bottom rail is ground. Label the top nodes, from left to right, \(A\), \(B\), \(C\), and \(D\).

The branches are as follows:
- an independent \(3\,\mathrm{A}\) current source occupying the entire left vertical, with its arrow directed upward from ground to \(A\);
- a \(2\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(B\);
- a \(4\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to ground (shared by the left and middle windows);
- a \(5\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to \(C\);
- an independent \(1\,\mathrm{A}\) current source from \(C\) to ground, with its arrow directed downward (shared by the middle and right windows);
- a \(6\,\Omega\) resistor from \(C\) to \(D\);
- an independent \(9\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from ground to \(D\), with the positive terminal at \(D\).

Take clockwise mesh currents \(I_1\), \(I_2\), and \(I_3\) in the left, middle, and right windows.

(a) Write every constraint that an independent current source imposes on the mesh currents, including the perimeter source that occupies a single window side.
(b) Write the supermesh KVL equation for the union of the middle and right windows: the clockwise closed walk through the \(4\,\Omega\) resistor (ground to \(B\)), the \(5\,\Omega\) resistor (\(B\) to \(C\)), the \(6\,\Omega\) resistor (\(C\) to \(D\)), and the \(9\,\mathrm{V}\) source (\(D\) to ground).
(c) Solve for \(I_1\), \(I_2\), and \(I_3\), and find the current directed from \(B\) to \(C\).

CIR:9908072.statement:
A connected planar ladder occupies three adjacent rectangular windows. The bottom rail is ground. Label the top nodes, from left to right, \(A\), \(B\), \(C\), and \(D\).

The branches are as follows:
- an independent \(24\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from ground to \(A\), with the positive terminal at \(A\);
- a \(3\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(B\);
- an independent \(1\,\mathrm{A}\) current source from \(B\) to ground, with its arrow directed downward (shared by the left and middle windows);
- a \(4\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to \(C\);
- an independent \(2\,\mathrm{A}\) current source from \(C\) to ground, with its arrow directed downward (shared by the middle and right windows);
- a \(5\,\Omega\) resistor from \(C\) to \(D\);
- a \(6\,\Omega\) resistor from \(D\) to ground.

Take clockwise mesh currents \(I_1\), \(I_2\), and \(I_3\) in the left, middle, and right windows.

(a) Explain why a single supermesh enclosing all three windows is required, and write the supermesh KVL equation for the clockwise outer walk through the \(24\,\mathrm{V}\) source, the \(3\,\Omega\) resistor, the \(4\,\Omega\) resistor, the \(5\,\Omega\) resistor, and the \(6\,\Omega\) resistor.
(b) Write the two auxiliary constraints imposed by the current sources.
(c) Solve for \(I_1\), \(I_2\), and \(I_3\).

CIR:9908073.statement:
A connected planar circuit occupies two adjacent rectangular windows. The bottom rail is ground. Label the top-left node \(A\) and the top-right node \(B\).

The branches are as follows:
- an independent \(16\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from ground to \(A\), with the positive terminal at \(A\);
- a \(4\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(B\);
- a \(2\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to ground (the shared vertical branch);
- an \(8\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to an intermediate right-hand node \(C\);
- a current-controlled voltage source occupying the remaining right vertical from ground to \(C\), with the positive terminal at \(C\) and value \(5 i_x\), where the controlling current \(i_x\) is the current through the \(4\,\Omega\) resistor directed from \(A\) to \(B\). The transresistance is \(5\,\Omega\).

Take clockwise mesh current \(I_1\) in the left window and clockwise mesh current \(I_2\) in the right window.

(a) Express \(i_x\) in terms of the mesh currents, and write the two mesh-current KVL equations.
(b) Solve for \(I_1\) and \(I_2\).
(c) Determine the voltage of node \(C\) with respect to ground.

CIR:9908074.statement:
A connected planar ladder occupies three adjacent rectangular windows. The bottom rail is ground. Label the top nodes, from left to right, \(A\), \(B\), \(C\), and \(D\).

The branches are as follows:
- an independent \(15\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from ground to \(A\), with the positive terminal at \(A\);
- a \(5\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(B\);
- a \(10\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to ground;
- a \(4\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to \(C\);
- a \(8\,\Omega\) resistor from \(C\) to ground;
- a \(2\,\Omega\) resistor from \(C\) to \(D\);
- a voltage-controlled voltage source occupying the entire right vertical from ground to \(D\), with the positive terminal at \(D\) and value \(2 v_x\), where \(v_x\) is the voltage across the \(10\,\Omega\) resistor with positive polarity at \(B\) (so \(v_x=v_B\)). The voltage gain is the dimensionless factor \(2\).

Take clockwise mesh currents \(I_1\), \(I_2\), and \(I_3\) in the left, middle, and right windows.

(a) Express \(v_x\) in terms of mesh currents, and write the three mesh-current KVL equations.
(b) Solve for \(I_1\), \(I_2\), and \(I_3\).
(c) Determine \(v_D\) with respect to ground.

CIR:9908075.statement:
A connected planar circuit occupies two adjacent rectangular windows whose shared vertical branch is a current-controlled current source. The bottom rail is ground. Label the top-left node \(A\), the top of the shared source \(P\), and the top-right node \(B\).

The branches are as follows:
- an independent \(9\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from ground to \(A\), with the positive terminal at \(A\);
- a \(3\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(P\);
- a current-controlled current source occupying the entire shared vertical from \(P\) to ground, with its arrow directed downward and value \(2 i_x\), where \(i_x\) is the current through the \(3\,\Omega\) resistor directed from \(A\) to \(P\). The current gain is the dimensionless factor \(2\);
- a \(6\,\Omega\) resistor from \(P\) to \(B\);
- a \(4\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to ground.

Take clockwise mesh current \(I_1\) in the left window (through the \(9\,\mathrm{V}\) source, the \(3\,\Omega\) resistor, and the controlled source) and clockwise mesh current \(I_2\) in the right window (through the controlled source, the \(6\,\Omega\) resistor, and the \(4\,\Omega\) resistor).

(a) Express \(i_x\) in terms of mesh currents, write the supermesh KVL equation for the clockwise outer walk through the \(9\,\mathrm{V}\) source, the \(3\,\Omega\) resistor, the \(6\,\Omega\) resistor, and the \(4\,\Omega\) resistor, and write the auxiliary constraint imposed by the controlled source.
(b) Solve for \(I_1\) and \(I_2\).
(c) Determine the voltage \(v_P\) with respect to ground.

CIR:9908076.statement:
A connected planar circuit occupies two adjacent rectangular windows. The bottom rail is ground. Label the top-left node \(A\), the top-right node \(B\), and an intermediate node \(C\) on the right vertical.

The branches are as follows:
- a voltage-controlled current source occupying the entire left vertical, with its arrow directed upward from ground to \(A\) and value \(0.25 v_x\). The transconductance is \(0.25\,\mathrm{S}\);
- a \(2\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(B\);
- a \(4\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to ground (the shared vertical branch);
- an \(8\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to \(C\);
- an independent \(10\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from ground to \(C\), with the positive terminal at \(C\).

The controlling voltage \(v_x\) is the voltage across the \(8\,\Omega\) resistor with positive polarity at \(B\), so \(v_x=v_B-v_C\).

Take clockwise mesh current \(I_1\) in the left window and clockwise mesh current \(I_2\) in the right window.

(a) Express the left-hand mesh current directly from the VCCS, express \(v_x\) in terms of \(I_2\), and write the right-hand mesh-current KVL equation.
(b) Solve for \(I_1\) and \(I_2\).
(c) Determine \(v_A\) and \(v_B\) with respect to ground.

CIR:9908077.statement:
A connected planar circuit occupies two adjacent rectangular windows whose shared vertical branch is a voltage-controlled voltage source. The bottom rail is ground. Label the top-left node \(A\), the top of the shared source \(B\), and the top-right node \(C\).

The branches are as follows:
- an independent \(12\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from ground to \(A\), with the positive terminal at \(A\);
- a \(3\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(B\);
- a voltage-controlled voltage source occupying the entire shared vertical from ground to \(B\), with the positive terminal at \(B\) and value \(0.5 v_x\), where \(v_x\) is the voltage across the \(3\,\Omega\) resistor with positive polarity at \(A\) (so \(v_x=v_A-v_B\)). The voltage gain is the dimensionless factor \(0.5\);
- a \(6\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to \(C\);
- a \(2\,\Omega\) resistor from \(C\) to ground.

Take clockwise mesh current \(I_1\) in the left window and clockwise mesh current \(I_2\) in the right window. The current through the controlled source, directed downward, is \(I_1-I_2\).

(a) Express \(v_x\) in terms of mesh currents, and write the two mesh-current KVL equations.
(b) Solve for \(I_1\) and \(I_2\).
(c) Determine \(v_B\) and the current leaving the positive terminal of the controlled source.

CIR:9908078.statement:
A connected planar circuit occupies two adjacent rectangular windows. The bottom rail is ground. Label the top-left node \(A\), the top of the shared vertical \(P\), the top-right node \(B\), and an intermediate node \(C\) on the right vertical.

The branches are as follows:
- an independent \(8\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from ground to \(A\), with the positive terminal at \(A\);
- a \(2\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(P\);
- a \(4\,\Omega\) resistor from \(P\) to ground (the shared vertical);
- a \(5\,\Omega\) resistor from \(P\) to \(B\);
- a current-controlled voltage source from \(C\) to \(B\), with the positive terminal at \(B\) and value \(3 i_y\), where \(i_y\) is the current through the \(4\,\Omega\) resistor directed downward. The transresistance is \(3\,\Omega\);
- a voltage-controlled current source occupying the remaining right vertical from \(C\) to ground, with its arrow directed downward and value \(0.2 v_z\), where \(v_z\) is the voltage across the \(2\,\Omega\) resistor with positive polarity at \(A\). The transconductance is \(0.2\,\mathrm{S}\).

Take clockwise mesh current \(I_1\) in the left window and clockwise mesh current \(I_2\) in the right window. The VCCS occupies the lower right vertical, so \(I_2\) is constrained by that source.

(a) Write the VCCS constraint on \(I_2\), express \(i_y\) and \(v_z\) in terms of mesh currents, and write the left-window mesh-current KVL equation.
(b) Solve for \(I_1\) and \(I_2\).
(c) Write the right-window KVL equation treating the voltage across the VCCS as unknown, and determine \(v_C\) and \(v_B\) with respect to ground.

CIR:9908079.statement:
A connected planar circuit occupies three adjacent rectangular windows. The bottom rail is ground. Label the top nodes, from left to right, \(A\), \(B\), \(C\), and \(D\).

The branches are as follows:
- an independent \(21\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from ground to \(A\), with the positive terminal at \(A\);
- a \(3\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(B\);
- a \(6\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to ground;
- a \(4\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to \(C\);
- a \(12\,\Omega\) resistor from \(C\) to ground;
- a \(2\,\Omega\) resistor from \(C\) to \(D\);
- a current-controlled voltage source occupying the right vertical from ground to \(D\), with the positive terminal at \(D\) and value \(4 i_w\), where \(i_w\) is the current through the \(6\,\Omega\) resistor directed downward. The transresistance is \(4\,\Omega\).

Take clockwise mesh currents \(I_1\), \(I_2\), and \(I_3\) in the left, middle, and right windows.

(a) Express \(i_w\) as a difference of mesh currents, and write the three mesh-current KVL equations.
(b) Solve for \(I_1\), \(I_2\), and \(I_3\).
(c) Determine the current directed from \(B\) to \(C\) and the voltage \(v_D\).

CIR:9908080.statement:
A connected planar circuit occupies two adjacent rectangular windows whose shared vertical branch is an independent current source, and whose right vertical branch is a voltage-controlled voltage source. The bottom rail is ground. Label the top-left node \(A\), the top of the shared source \(B\), and the top-right node \(C\).

The branches are as follows:
- an independent \(10\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from ground to \(A\), with the positive terminal at \(A\);
- a \(5\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(B\);
- an independent \(0.5\,\mathrm{A}\) current source from \(B\) to ground, with its arrow directed downward;
- a \(8\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to \(C\);
- a voltage-controlled voltage source occupying the right vertical from ground to \(C\), with the positive terminal at \(C\) and value \(1.5 v_x\), where \(v_x\) is the voltage across the \(5\,\Omega\) resistor with positive polarity at \(A\). The voltage gain is the dimensionless factor \(1.5\).

Take clockwise mesh current \(I_1\) in the left window and clockwise mesh current \(I_2\) in the right window.

(a) Write the supermesh KVL equation for the clockwise outer walk through the \(10\,\mathrm{V}\) source, the \(5\,\Omega\) resistor, the \(8\,\Omega\) resistor, and the VCVS, together with the current-source constraint on \(I_1\) and \(I_2\). Express \(v_x\) in terms of mesh currents inside those equations.
(b) Solve for \(I_1\) and \(I_2\).
(c) Determine \(v_C\) and the voltage across the independent current source, positive at \(B\) with respect to ground.

CIR:9908081.statement:
A connected planar ladder occupies three adjacent rectangular windows. The bottom rail is ground. Label the top nodes, from left to right, \(A\), \(B\), \(C\), and \(D\).

The branches are as follows:
- an independent \(14\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from ground to \(A\), with the positive terminal at \(A\);
- a \(2\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(B\);
- an independent \(1\,\mathrm{A}\) current source from \(B\) to ground, with its arrow directed downward (shared by the left and middle windows);
- a \(3\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to \(C\);
- a \(6\,\Omega\) resistor from \(C\) to ground (shared by the middle and right windows);
- a \(4\,\Omega\) resistor from \(C\) to \(D\);
- a current-controlled current source occupying the entire right vertical from \(D\) to ground, with its arrow directed downward and value \(0.5 i_u\), where \(i_u\) is the current through the \(2\,\Omega\) resistor directed from \(A\) to \(B\). The current gain is the dimensionless factor \(0.5\).

Take clockwise mesh currents \(I_1\), \(I_2\), and \(I_3\) in the left, middle, and right windows.

(a) Write the constraint imposed by the perimeter CCCS on \(I_3\), the constraint imposed by the shared independent current source on \(I_1\) and \(I_2\), and the supermesh KVL equation for the union of the left and middle windows (clockwise walk through the \(14\,\mathrm{V}\) source, the \(2\,\Omega\) resistor, the \(3\,\Omega\) resistor, and the \(6\,\Omega\) resistor).
(b) Solve for \(I_1\), \(I_2\), and \(I_3\).
(c) Determine \(v_D\) with respect to ground.

CIR:9908082.statement:
A connected planar circuit occupies two adjacent rectangular windows. The bottom rail is ground. Label the top-left node \(A\), the top-right node \(B\), and an intermediate node \(C\) on the right vertical.

The branches are as follows:
- an independent \(12\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from ground to \(A\), with the positive terminal at \(A\);
- a \(1\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(B\);
- a \(3\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to ground (the shared vertical);
- a \(2\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to \(C\);
- an independent \(6\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from ground to \(C\), with the positive terminal at \(C\).

Take clockwise mesh current \(I_1\) in the left window and clockwise mesh current \(I_2\) in the right window.

(a) Write the two mesh-current KVL equations and solve for \(I_1\) and \(I_2\).
(b) For every element, compute the absorbed power using the passive-sign convention: \(p=vi\) if and only if the referenced current enters the terminal that is positive for the referenced voltage. Tabulate the absorbed power of each of the three resistors and of each of the two voltage sources.
(c) Verify that the algebraic sum of all five absorbed powers is zero, and state the total power delivered by sources and the total power absorbed by resistors.

CIR:9908083.statement:
A connected planar circuit occupies two adjacent rectangular windows. The bottom rail is ground. Label the top-left node \(A\) and the top-right node \(B\).

The branches are as follows:
- an independent \(18\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from ground to \(A\), with the positive terminal at \(A\);
- a \(6\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(B\);
- a \(3\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to ground (shared vertical);
- a current-controlled voltage source occupying the right vertical from ground to \(B\), with the positive terminal at \(B\) and value \(2 i_x\), where \(i_x\) is the current through the \(6\,\Omega\) resistor directed from \(A\) to \(B\). The transresistance is \(2\,\Omega\).

Take clockwise mesh current \(I_1\) in the left window and clockwise mesh current \(I_2\) in the right window.

(a) Write the two mesh-current KVL equations, with \(i_x\) expressed in terms of mesh currents, and solve for \(I_1\) and \(I_2\).
(b) Compute the absorbed power of each resistor and of each source (the independent source and the CCVS) under the passive-sign convention.
(c) Verify that the algebraic sum of all absorbed powers is zero. State whether the CCVS absorbs or delivers power at this operating point.

CIR:9908084.statement:
A connected planar circuit occupies two adjacent rectangular windows whose shared vertical branch is an independent current source. The bottom rail is ground. Label the top-left node \(A\), the top of the shared source \(B\), and the top-right node \(C\).

The branches are as follows:
- an independent \(15\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from ground to \(A\), with the positive terminal at \(A\);
- a \(4\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(B\);
- an independent \(3\,\mathrm{A}\) current source from \(B\) to ground, with its arrow directed downward;
- a \(2\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to \(C\);
- a \(6\,\Omega\) resistor from \(C\) to ground.

Take clockwise mesh current \(I_1\) in the left window and clockwise mesh current \(I_2\) in the right window.

(a) Write the supermesh KVL equation and the current-source constraint, and solve for \(I_1\) and \(I_2\).
(b) Determine the voltage across the current source, positive at \(B\) with respect to ground, by using resistor drops (or the already written supermesh relation). Then compute the absorbed power of the \(15\,\mathrm{V}\) source, of the \(3\,\mathrm{A}\) source, and of each resistor, using the passive-sign convention.
(c) Verify that the algebraic sum of all absorbed powers is zero.

CIR:9908085.statement:
A connected planar ladder occupies three adjacent rectangular windows. The bottom rail is ground. Label the top nodes, from left to right, \(A\), \(B\), \(C\), and \(D\).

The branches are as follows:
- an independent \(12\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from ground to \(A\), with the positive terminal at \(A\);
- a \(2\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(B\);
- a \(4\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to ground;
- a \(6\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to \(C\);
- a \(3\,\Omega\) resistor from \(C\) to ground;
- a \(1\,\Omega\) resistor from \(C\) to \(D\);
- a voltage-controlled voltage source occupying the right vertical from ground to \(D\), with the positive terminal at \(D\) and value \(3 v_x\), where \(v_x\) is the voltage across the \(4\,\Omega\) resistor with positive polarity at \(B\). The voltage gain is the dimensionless factor \(3\).

Take clockwise mesh currents \(I_1\), \(I_2\), and \(I_3\) in the left, middle, and right windows.

(a) Write the three mesh-current KVL equations, with \(v_x\) expressed in terms of mesh currents, and solve for \(I_1\), \(I_2\), and \(I_3\).
(b) Compute the absorbed power of every resistor, of the independent voltage source, and of the VCVS, using the passive-sign convention.
(c) Verify exact power balance (the algebraic sum of absorbed powers equals zero) and identify which sources deliver power.

CIR:9908086.statement:
A connected planar circuit occupies three adjacent rectangular windows. The bottom rail is ground. Label the top nodes, from left to right, \(A\), \(B\), and \(C\).

The branches are as follows:
- an independent \(24\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from ground to \(A\), with the positive terminal at \(A\);
- an \(8\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(B\);
- a \(4\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to ground (shared by the left and middle windows);
- a \(12\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to ground (shared by the middle and right windows);
- an ideal wire from \(B\) to \(C\);
- an independent \(4\,\mathrm{A}\) current source from \(C\) to ground, with its arrow directed downward (the entire right vertical).

Take clockwise mesh current \(I_1\) in the left window, clockwise \(I_2\) in the middle window, and clockwise \(I_3\) in the right window.

The right-hand mesh current is constrained by the perimeter current source. The closed walk through the \(24\,\mathrm{V}\) source, the \(8\,\Omega\) resistor, and the \(12\,\Omega\) resistor is a non-window loop.

(a) Write every independent mesh or supermesh equation required by this embedding, including the constraint from the \(4\,\mathrm{A}\) source, and solve for \(I_1\), \(I_2\), and \(I_3\).
(b) As a KVL cross-check that is not a window mesh, write the loop equation for the non-window closed walk through the \(24\,\mathrm{V}\) source, the \(8\,\Omega\) resistor, and the \(12\,\Omega\) resistor, and verify that it is satisfied by the mesh solution.
(c) Compute every element’s absorbed power under the passive-sign convention and verify that the powers sum to zero.

CIR:9908087.statement:
A connected planar circuit occupies two adjacent rectangular windows. The bottom rail is ground. Label the top-left node \(A\) and the top-right node \(B\).

The branches are as follows:
- an independent \(6\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from ground to \(A\), with the positive terminal at \(A\);
- a \(2\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(B\);
- a \(5\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to ground (shared vertical);
- a \(3\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to an intermediate node \(C\);
- an independent \(4\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from ground to \(C\), with the positive terminal at \(C\).

A proposed analysis assigns a single figure-eight current \(I_{\mathrm{fig}}\) that travels clockwise around the left window and then anticlockwise around the right window, treating that figure-eight as one mesh.

(a) Explain, in terms of planar embeddings and independent window cycles, why \(I_{\mathrm{fig}}\) is not a valid single mesh current for this circuit, and why the two bounded faces still require two mesh currents.
(b) Replace the figure-eight assignment by clockwise window currents \(I_1\) (left) and \(I_2\) (right), write the two independent KVL equations, and solve for \(I_1\) and \(I_2\).
(c) Determine the current directed from \(A\) to \(B\) and the voltage \(v_B\) with respect to ground.

CIR:9908089.statement:
A connected planar ladder occupies three adjacent rectangular windows. The bottom rail is ground. Label the top nodes, from left to right, \(A\), \(B\), \(C\), and \(D\).

The branches are as follows:
- an independent \(16\,\mathrm{V}\) voltage source from ground to \(A\), with the positive terminal at \(A\);
- a \(4\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(B\);
- an independent \(2\,\mathrm{A}\) current source from \(B\) to ground, with its arrow directed downward (shared by the left and middle windows);
- a \(5\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to \(C\);
- a \(10\,\Omega\) resistor from \(C\) to ground (shared by the middle and right windows);
- a \(8\,\Omega\) resistor from \(C\) to \(D\);
- a current-controlled voltage source occupying the right vertical from ground to \(D\), with the positive terminal at \(D\) and value \(6 i_x\), where \(i_x\) is the current through the \(4\,\Omega\) resistor directed from \(A\) to \(B\). The transresistance is \(6\,\Omega\).

Take clockwise mesh currents \(I_1\), \(I_2\), and \(I_3\) in the left, middle, and right windows.

(a) Write the supermesh KVL equation for the union of the left and middle windows, the ordinary mesh-current KVL equation for the right window, and the auxiliary constraint from the \(2\,\mathrm{A}\) source. Express \(i_x\) in terms of mesh currents throughout.
(b) Solve for \(I_1\), \(I_2\), and \(I_3\). Then determine the voltage across the current source, positive at \(B\) with respect to ground, and the voltage \(v_D\).
(c) Using the passive-sign convention, compute the absorbed power of every resistor, of the independent voltage source, of the independent current source, and of the CCVS. Verify that the algebraic sum of all absorbed powers is identically zero.

CIR:9908299.statement:
A connected resistive network has essential nodes \(A\), \(B\), \(C\) and reference node \(N\) (ground). Node voltages are with respect to \(N\). The branches are:
- an independent voltage source of \(60.0\,\mathrm{V}\) from \(N\) to \(A\), with its positive terminal at \(A\);
- a \(5.00\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(B\);
- a \(20.0\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to \(N\);
- a \(10.0\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(C\);
- a \(15.0\,\Omega\) resistor from \(C\) to \(N\);
- a \(4.00\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to \(C\);
- an independent current source of \(2.50\,\mathrm{A}\) from \(C\) to \(B\), with its reference arrow directed from \(C\) toward \(B\).

No other elements are present.

(a) With \(v_A\) known from the voltage source, write independent KCL equations at \(B\) and at \(C\), taking currents leaving each of those nodes through resistors as positive and retaining the \(2.50\,\mathrm{A}\) source with the stated direction. Solve for \(v_B\) and \(v_C\) in volts.

(b) Determine every resistor current with a declared direction: \(A\) to \(B\) through \(5.00\,\Omega\), \(B\) to \(N\) through \(20.0\,\Omega\), \(A\) to \(C\) through \(10.0\,\Omega\), \(C\) to \(N\) through \(15.0\,\Omega\), and \(B\) to \(C\) through \(4.00\,\Omega\). Determine the current through the \(60.0\,\mathrm{V}\) source, positive from \(A\) through the source toward \(N\).

(c) Using the passive sign convention, compute the absorbed power of every element (five resistors and two independent sources). Verify that the algebraic sum of all absorbed powers is zero, and identify which sources deliver power.

(d) Write KCL at \(N\) with currents leaving \(N\) taken as positive, and confirm that it is satisfied by the solution of (a)–(b). State why this equation is not independent of the KCL statements already used.

CIR:9908300.statement:
A connected resistive network has essential nodes \(A\), \(B\), \(C\) and reference node \(N\) (ground). The branches are:
- an independent voltage source of \(20.0\,\mathrm{V}\) from \(N\) to \(A\), with its positive terminal at \(A\);
- a \(5.00\,\Omega\) resistor from \(A\) to \(B\); define the controlling current \(i_x\) as the current in this resistor, positive from \(A\) toward \(B\);
- a \(10.0\,\Omega\) resistor from \(B\) to \(N\);
- a current-controlled voltage source from \(B\) to \(C\), with its negative terminal at \(B\), its positive terminal at \(C\), and value \(3.00\,i_x\) (ohms times amperes), so that \(v_C-v_B=3.00\,i_x\);
- an \(8.00\,\Omega\) resistor from \(C\) to \(N\);
- a voltage-controlled current source from \(N\) to \(B\), with its reference arrow directed from \(N\) toward \(B\) and value \(0.200\,v_C\) amperes, where \(v_C\) is in volts. The transconductance is \(0.200\,\mathrm{S}\).

No other elements are present. Node voltages are with respect to \(N\).

(a) Identify the supernode associated with the CCVS. Express \(i_x\) in terms of \(v_B\), write the CCVS constraint relating \(v_C\) and \(v_B\), and write supernode KCL for \(\{B,C\}\), counting both the independent-source current into \(A\) (through the \(5.00\,\Omega\) resistor) and the VCCS current into \(B\).

(b) Solve for \(v_B\), \(v_C\), and \(i_x\). Then determine the current through the CCVS, positive from \(C\) through the CCVS toward \(B\), by KCL at \(C\), and determine the VCCS current through its declared arrow.

(c) Using the passive sign convention, compute the absorbed power of the independent voltage source, of the CCVS, of the VCCS, and of each of the three resistors. Verify that the algebraic sum of all absorbed powers is zero. State whether each dependent source absorbs or delivers power at this operating point.

(d) Arrange the independent nodal/constraint equations as a linear system in \((v_B,v_C)\). Identify every matrix entry produced by a dependent source, and state whether the coefficient matrix is symmetric.

CIR:9909068.statement:
A connected network between terminals \(a\) and \(b\) consists of exactly five branches and two internal nodes \(c\) and \(d\):
- a \(4.00\,\Omega\) resistor from \(a\) to \(c\);
- a \(j6.00\,\Omega\) inductor from \(c\) to \(d\);
- a \(-j8.00\,\Omega\) capacitor from \(d\) to \(b\);
- a \(10.0\,\Omega\) resistor from \(a\) to \(d\);
- a \(5.00\,\Omega\) resistor from \(c\) to \(b\).

No other branch is present. The impedances already incorporate the operating frequency. Successive series--parallel reduction is not available. All phasors are cosine-referenced RMS phasors; constant phases are in degrees.

