APAB:9913012.canonicalSolution:
Direct substitution yields \(0/0\). For \(x\neq 7\) in the domain \(x\ge -2\),
\begin{align*}
\frac{\sqrt{x+2}-3}{x-7}
\cdot\frac{\sqrt{x+2}+3}{\sqrt{x+2}+3}
&=\frac{(x+2)-9}{(x-7)\bigl(\sqrt{x+2}+3\bigr)}
=\frac{x-7}{(x-7)\bigl(\sqrt{x+2}+3\bigr)}
=\frac{1}{\sqrt{x+2}+3}.
\end{align*}
The factor \(\sqrt{x+2}+3\ge 3>0\) never vanishes, so multiplying by the conjugate does not change the value. Cancelling \(x-7\) is valid only for \(x\neq 7\) and does not claim that the original quotient is defined at \(x=7\). The limit of the original (undefined at \(7\)) equals the value of the simplified continuous function:
\[
\lim_{x\to 7}\frac{\sqrt{x+2}-3}{x-7}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{9}+3}=\frac{1}{6}.
\]

**Final answer.** \(1/6\)

