APAB:9913174.canonicalSolution:
(a) \(f'(x)=3x^2-6x-9=3(x^2-2x-3)=3(x-3)(x+1)\). Then \(f'(x)>0\) for \(x<-1\) and for \(x>3\), while \(f'(x)<0\) for \(-1<x<3\). Thus \(f\) is increasing on \((-\infty,-1)\) and on \((3,\infty)\), and decreasing on \((-1,3)\). The first-derivative test classifies \(x=-1\) as a local maximum, with \(f(-1)=5\), and \(x=3\) as a local minimum, with \(f(3)=-27\).

(b) \(f''(x)=6x-6=6(x-1)\). So \(f''(x)<0\) on \((-\infty,1)\) (concave down) and \(f''(x)>0\) on \((1,\infty)\) (concave up). The only inflection point is \(\bigl(1,f(1)\bigr)=(1,-11)\).

(c) Sign chart for \(f'\), reconstructed from the factored form \(3(x-3)(x+1)\):
- \(x<-1\): both factors negative, \(f'>0\);
- \(-1<x<3\): \((x+1)>0>(x-3)\), \(f'<0\);
- \(x>3\): both factors positive, \(f'>0\).

Sign chart for \(f''\), reconstructed from \(6(x-1)\):
- \(x<1\): \(f''<0\);
- \(x>1\): \(f''>0\).

Final answer: incr. \((-\infty,-1)\cup(3,\infty)\), decr. \((-1,3)\); loc. max \((-1,5)\), loc. min \((3,-27)\); CD on \((-\infty,1)\), CU on \((1,\infty)\); inflection \((1,-11)\)

