APBC:9914229.canonicalSolution:
(a) The base case \(a_1=3\ge\sqrt{5}\) is immediate. Assuming \(a_n\ge\sqrt{5}\) (and hence \(a_n>0\)),
\begin{align*}
a_{n+1}-\sqrt{5}
&=\frac12\Bigl(a_n+\frac5{a_n}\Bigr)-\sqrt{5}
=\frac{a_n^2-2\sqrt{5}\,a_n+5}{2a_n}
=\frac{(a_n-\sqrt{5})^2}{2a_n}\ge 0,
\end{align*}
so \(a_{n+1}\ge\sqrt{5}\). Thus \(a_n\ge\sqrt{5}\) for every \(n\ge 1\).

The same computation shows that \(a_{n+1}\le a_n\) if and only if \(a_n/2+5/(2a_n)\le a_n\), i.e. \(5/a_n\le a_n\), i.e. \(a_n^2\ge 5\). Combined with positivity this is exactly \(a_n\ge\sqrt{5}\), which has already been proved. Hence \((a_n)\) is decreasing and bounded below, so it converges to some \(L\ge\sqrt{5}\). Passing to the limit in the recurrence yields \(L=\frac12(L+5/L)\), or \(L^2=5\). The condition \(L\ge\sqrt{5}\) forces \(L=\sqrt{5}\).

(b) The identity \(a_{n+1}-\sqrt{5}=(a_n-\sqrt{5})^2/(2a_n)\) is precisely the algebraic rearrangement recorded in (a).

(c) From (a) one has \(a_n\ge\sqrt{5}>0\), so \(2a_n\ge 2\sqrt{5}\) and therefore
\[
0\le a_{n+1}-\sqrt{5}=\frac{(a_n-\sqrt{5})^2}{2a_n}\le\frac{(a_n-\sqrt{5})^2}{2\sqrt{5}}.
\]
A one-step geometric bound would control the error \(e_{n+1}:=a_{n+1}-\sqrt{5}\) by a fixed ratio \(r\in(0,1)\) times \(e_n\). The estimate of this part is quadratic: \(e_{n+1}\le e_n^2/(2\sqrt{5})\). Once \(e_n\) is smaller than \(1\), one has \(e_n^2\ll e_n\), so the next error is of strictly smaller order than any fixed linear contraction of the current error. That is the sense in which the bound is strictly stronger than a one-step geometric estimate near the limit.

**Final answer.** \(a_n\ge\sqrt{5}\), decreasing, \(L=\sqrt{5}\); \(a_{n+1}-\sqrt{5}=(a_n-\sqrt{5})^2/(2a_n)\); quadratic bound via \(2a_n\ge 2\sqrt{5}\)

