APBC:9914231.canonicalSolution:
(a) \(a_1=S_1=3\cdot 1/(1+2)=1\). For \(n\ge 2\),
\begin{align*}
a_n=S_n-S_{n-1}
&=\frac{3n}{n+2}-\frac{3(n-1)}{n+1}
=3\cdot\frac{n(n+1)-(n-1)(n+2)}{(n+2)(n+1)}
=3\cdot\frac{2}{(n+1)(n+2)}
=\frac{6}{(n+1)(n+2)}.
\end{align*}

(b) \(\lim_{n\to\infty}S_n=3\), so the series converges and its sum is \(3\).

(c) \(R_N=S-S_N=3-3N/(N+2)=6/(N+2)\). The inequality \(R_N<0.01\) is \(6/(N+2)<1/100\), or \(N+2>600\), or \(N>598\). For \(N=598\) one has \(R_{598}=6/600=0.01\), which is not strictly smaller. Thus the smallest positive integer is \(N=599\).

(d) Partial fractions give \(1/((n+1)(n+2))=1/(n+1)-1/(n+2)\). Hence
\[
\sum_{n=2}^N a_n=6\sum_{n=2}^N\Bigl(\frac1{n+1}-\frac1{n+2}\Bigr)=6\Bigl(\frac13-\frac1{N+2}\Bigr)=2-\frac{6}{N+2}.
\]
Adding \(a_1=1\) produces
\[
a_1+\sum_{n=2}^N a_n=3-\frac{6}{N+2}=\frac{3N}{N+2}=S_N,
\]
