The original expression is undefined wherever \(x^{2}-9=0\) or \(x-3=0\), i.e. at \(x=3\) and at \(x=-3\). For every other \(x\),
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{x-3}-\frac{6}{x^{2}-9}
&=\frac{1}{x-3}-\frac{6}{(x-3)(x+3)}
=\frac{x+3-6}{(x-3)(x+3)}
=\frac{x-3}{(x-3)(x+3)}
=\frac{1}{x+3}.
\end{align*}
The cancellation requires \(x\neq 3\); the identity also still excludes \(x=-3\). The cancelled function \(1/(x+3)\) is continuous at \(x=3\), so
\[
\lim_{x\to 3}\left(\frac{1}{x-3}-\frac{6}{x^{2}-9}\right)=\frac{1}{6}.
\]
The two-sided limit can exist at a point where the original formula is undefined because a limit never consults the value (or the lack of a value) at the point itself. On a punctured neighborhood of \(3\) that does not reach \(-3\), the original expression agrees with the continuous function \(1/(x+3)\). The singularity at \(x=3\) is a hole, not a vertical asymptote.

