CALC1:9915227.canonicalSolution:
**(a)** \(\sin x=\dfrac{1}{2}\) on \([0,\pi]\) at the reference angle \(\dfrac{\pi}{6}\) in Quadrants I and II, hence at \(x=\dfrac{\pi}{6}\) and \(x=\dfrac{5\pi}{6}\).

**(b)** The sine function on \([0,\pi]\) rises from \(0\) to \(1\) and falls from \(1\) to \(0\), so \(\sin x\ge\dfrac{1}{2}\) precisely on the closed interval \(\bigl[\dfrac{\pi}{6},\dfrac{5\pi}{6}\bigr]\).

**(c)**
\begin{align*}
\operatorname{Area}
&=\int_{\pi/6}^{5\pi/6}\Bigl(\sin x-\frac{1}{2}\Bigr)\,dx
=\Bigl[-\cos x-\frac{x}{2}\Bigr]_{\pi/6}^{5\pi/6}\\
&=\Bigl(-\cos\frac{5\pi}{6}-\frac{5\pi}{12}\Bigr)-\Bigl(-\cos\frac{\pi}{6}-\frac{\pi}{12}\Bigr)\\
&=\Bigl(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}-\frac{5\pi}{12}\Bigr)-\Bigl(-\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}-\frac{\pi}{12}\Bigr)
=\sqrt{3}-\frac{4\pi}{12}
=\sqrt{3}-\frac{\pi}{3}.
\end{align*}

**Final answer.** (a) \(\pi/6\), \(5\pi/6\). (b) \([\pi/6,5\pi/6]\). (c) \(\sqrt{3}-\pi/3\).

