(a) Set \(x=3\tan\theta\). Then \(\theta\) runs from \(\arctan 1=\pi/4\) to \(\arctan\sqrt{3}=\pi/3\). Also \(dx=3\sec^2\theta\,d\theta\), \(x^2=9\tan^2\theta\), and \(\sqrt{x^2+9}=3\sec\theta\). The integrand becomes
\[
\frac{3\sec^2\theta\,d\theta}{9\tan^2\theta\cdot 3\sec\theta}=\frac{\sec\theta}{9\tan^2\theta}\,d\theta=\frac{1}{9}\cdot\frac{\cos\theta}{\sin^2\theta}\,d\theta=\frac19\csc\theta\cot\theta\,d\theta.
\]

(b) An antiderivative is \(-\frac19\csc\theta\). Evaluating in \(\theta\),
\[
-\frac19\Bigl[\csc\theta\Bigr]_{\pi/4}^{\pi/3}=-\frac19\Bigl(\frac{2}{\sqrt{3}}-\sqrt{2}\Bigr)=\frac{\sqrt{2}}{9}-\frac{2}{9\sqrt{3}}.
\]

(c) In the right triangle with opposite \(x\), adjacent \(3\), and hypotenuse \(\sqrt{x^2+9}\), one has \(\csc\theta=\sqrt{x^2+9}/x\). Thus an antiderivative in \(x\) is \(-\dfrac{\sqrt{x^2+9}}{9x}\). Evaluating at the original endpoints,
\begin{align*}
\Bigl[-\frac{\sqrt{x^2+9}}{9x}\Bigr]_3^{3\sqrt{3}}
&=-\frac{\sqrt{36}}{27\sqrt{3}}+\frac{\sqrt{18}}{27}
=-\frac{6}{27\sqrt{3}}+\frac{3\sqrt{2}}{27}
=\frac{\sqrt{2}}{9}-\frac{2}{9\sqrt{3}}
=\frac{3\sqrt{2}-2\sqrt{3}}{27}.
\end{align*}

**Final answer.** \(\dfrac{3\sqrt{2}-2\sqrt{3}}{27}\)

