CALC2:9916193.canonicalSolution:
**(a)** Starting from \(a_1=1\),
\begin{align*}
a_2&=1+\frac{1}{1+1}=\frac32,\\
a_3&=1+\frac{1}{1+\frac32}=1+\frac25=\frac75,\\
a_4&=1+\frac{1}{1+\frac75}=1+\frac{5}{12}=\frac{17}{12},\\
a_5&=1+\frac{1}{1+\frac{17}{12}}=1+\frac{12}{29}=\frac{41}{29}.
\end{align*}

**(b)** Substitute the recurrence twice:
\[
a_{n+1}=1+\frac{1}{1+a_n}=\frac{2+a_n}{1+a_n},
\]
then
\begin{align*}
a_{n+2}
&=1+\frac{1}{1+a_{n+1}}
=1+\frac{1}{1+\frac{2+a_n}{1+a_n}}
=1+\frac{1+a_n}{1+a_n+2+a_n}
=1+\frac{1+a_n}{2a_n+3}
=\frac{3a_n+4}{2a_n+3}.
\end{align*}
The first identity is then
\[
a_{n+2}-a_n=\frac{3a_n+4-a_n(2a_n+3)}{2a_n+3}=\frac{3a_n+4-2a_n^{2}-3a_n}{2a_n+3}=\frac{2(2-a_n^{2})}{2a_n+3}.
\]
The second is
\begin{align*}
a_{n+2}-\sqrt{2}
&=\frac{3a_n+4-\sqrt{2}(2a_n+3)}{2a_n+3}
=\frac{(3-2\sqrt{2})a_n+(4-3\sqrt{2})}{2a_n+3}.
\end{align*}
The numerator equals \((3-2\sqrt{2})(a_n-\sqrt{2})\) because
\[
(3-2\sqrt{2})\sqrt{2}=3\sqrt{2}-4,
\]
hence \(-(3-2\sqrt{2})\sqrt{2}=4-3\sqrt{2}\).

**(c)** All terms are positive (by a trivial induction from \(a_1=1>0\)), so the denominator \(2a_n+3\) is positive. Also \(3-2\sqrt{2}>0\) because \(9>8\). From the two identities:
- if \(a_n<\sqrt{2}\), then \(a_{n+2}>a_n\) and \(a_{n+2}<\sqrt{2}\);
- if \(a_n>\sqrt{2}\), then \(a_{n+2}<a_n\) and \(a_{n+2}>\sqrt{2}\).

The odd subsequence starts at \(a_1=1<\sqrt{2}\), so it is increasing and bounded above by \(\sqrt{2}\). The even subsequence starts at \(a_2=\frac32>\sqrt{2}\) (since \(\bigl(\frac32\bigr)^{2}=\frac94>2\)), so it is decreasing and bounded below by \(\sqrt{2}\).

**(d)** The odd subsequence is increasing and bounded above, hence converges to some \(L_{\mathrm{odd}}\le\sqrt{2}\). The even subsequence is decreasing and bounded below, hence converges to some \(L_{\mathrm{even}}\ge\sqrt{2}\). Passing to the limit in the two-step recurrence, each of these limits satisfies \(L=(3L+4)/(2L+3)\), or \(2L^{2}+3L=3L+4\), hence \(L^{2}=2\). The only nonnegative solution is \(L=\sqrt{2}\). Thus both subsequences converge to \(\sqrt{2}\), so \(a_n\to\sqrt{2}\).

**Final answer.** (a) \(\dfrac32,\dfrac75,\dfrac{17}{12},\dfrac{41}{29}\); (b) identities as stated; (c)–(d) \(a_n\to\sqrt{2}\)

