\begin{align*}
\frac{\bigl((t+h)^{2}-(t+h)\bigr)-(t^{2}-t)}{h}
&=\frac{t^{2}+2th+h^{2}-t-h-t^{2}+t}{h}
=\frac{2th+h^{2}-h}{h}=2t+h-1,
\end{align*}
which tends to \(2t-1\) as \(h\to 0\). The second is
\[
\frac{e^{2(t+h)}-e^{2t}}{h}=e^{2t}\cdot\frac{e^{2h}-1}{h}=2e^{2t}\cdot\frac{e^{2h}-1}{2h}\to 2e^{2t}\cdot 1,
\]
using \(\lim_{k\to 0}(e^{k}-1)/k=1\) with \(k=2h\). The third is
\begin{align*}
\frac{\cos(t+h)-\cos t}{h}
&=\frac{\cos t\,(\cos h-1)-\sin t\,\sin h}{h}
=\cos t\cdot\frac{\cos h-1}{h}-\sin t\cdot\frac{\sin h}{h}.
\end{align*}
As \(h\to 0\) one has \((\cos h-1)/h\to 0\) and \((\sin h)/h\to 1\), so the limit is \(-\sin t\). Therefore
\[
\mathbf{r}'(t)=\langle 2t-1,\ 2e^{2t},\ -\sin t\rangle.
\]

(b) \(\mathbf{r}'(0)=\langle -1,2,0\rangle\) and \(\mathbf{r}'(\pi/2)=\langle \pi-1,\ 2e^{\pi},\ -1\rangle\).

(c) The point is \(\mathbf{r}(0)=\langle 0,1,1\rangle\). Parametric equations of the tangent line:
\[
x=-s,\qquad y=1+2s,\qquad z=1.
\]

**Final answer.** (a) \(\langle 2t-1,\,2e^{2t},\,-\sin t\rangle\); (b) \(\langle -1,2,0\rangle\), \(\langle\pi-1,\,2e^{\pi},\,-1\rangle\); (c) \(x=-s\), \(y=1+2s\), \(z=1\)

