DLOG:9918113.statement:
An 8-bit address \(A_7A_6A_5A_4A_3A_2A_1A_0\) (\(A_7\) most significant) is decoded by a 3-to-8 decoder whose address pins receive \(A_7A_6A_5\) in that significance order, whose enable is the constant \(1\), and whose active-high outputs are \(Y_0,\ldots,Y_7\) corresponding to \(A_7A_6A_5=000,\ldots,111\). Four devices occupy the following exclusive address ranges, each to be selected by a single decoder output used as an active-high chip-select:

- device \(D0\): \(0\mathrm{x}00\)--\(0\mathrm{x}1\mathrm{F}\);
- device \(D1\): \(0\mathrm{x}20\)--\(0\mathrm{x}3\mathrm{F}\);
- device \(D2\): \(0\mathrm{x}40\)--\(0\mathrm{x}5\mathrm{F}\);
- device \(D3\): \(0\mathrm{x}80\)--\(0\mathrm{x}9\mathrm{F}\).

Addresses are written in hexadecimal; the decoder does not examine \(A_4,\ldots,A_0\).

(a) Identify which decoder output is the chip-select of each of \(D0,D1,D2,D3\). State which of \(Y_0,\ldots,Y_7\) are unused.

(b) For address \(0\mathrm{x}2\mathrm{C}\), list the four chip-selects \((CS_{D0},CS_{D1},CS_{D2},CS_{D3})\).

(c) For address \(0\mathrm{x}60\), list the same four chip-selects.

(d) The decoder enable is changed from the constant \(1\) to the address bit \(A_4\), with every other connection unchanged. Re-evaluate the four chip-selects at \(0\mathrm{x}2\mathrm{C}\) and at \(0\mathrm{x}20\).

DLOG:9918120.statement:
A \(2\times 2\) combinational switch has data inputs \(U,V\), data outputs \(P,Q\), and control word \(C_1C_0\). Four tri-state buffers are available:

- buffer UP drives \(P\) from data \(U\) when enabled by \(E_{\mathrm{UP}}\);
- buffer VP drives \(P\) from data \(V\) when enabled by \(E_{\mathrm{VP}}\);
- buffer UQ drives \(Q\) from data \(U\) when enabled by \(E_{\mathrm{UQ}}\);
- buffer VQ drives \(Q\) from data \(V\) when enabled by \(E_{\mathrm{VQ}}\).

The required function is:

- \(C_1C_0=00\): \(P=U\) and \(Q=V\) (straight);
- \(C_1C_0=01\): \(P=V\) and \(Q=U\) (exchange);
- \(C_1C_0=10\): \(P=U\) and \(Q=U\) (broadcast \(U\));
- \(C_1C_0=11\): both \(P\) and \(Q\) high-impedance.

Bus-value rules on each output: high-impedance if every driver of that output is off; the driven value if exactly one buffer is enabled or if two enabled buffers present the same value; contention \(C\) if two enabled buffers present opposite values. A weak pull-down on each output reads logic \(0\) if and only if that output is high-impedance.

(a) Write a sum-of-products expression for each of \(E_{\mathrm{UP}}\), \(E_{\mathrm{VP}}\), \(E_{\mathrm{UQ}}\), and \(E_{\mathrm{VQ}}\) in the variables \(C_1,C_0\) that meets the specification and never enables two buffers onto the same output with potentially different data. (Broadcast of \(U\) must not enable a \(V\)-driver on either output.)

(b) A 2-to-4 decoder with address \(C_1C_0\) (\(C_1\) more significant), enable tied to \(1\), and active-high outputs \(Y_0,Y_1,Y_2,Y_3\) corresponding to \(C_1C_0=00,01,10,11\) is available. Express each of the four enables as an OR of decoder outputs (an OR of a single output is just a wire).

(c) For each of the four control words, state \(P\) and \(Q\) in terms of \(U\) and \(V\), using the symbols \(U\), \(V\), \(Z\), \(0\) (after the pull-down), or \(C\) as appropriate.

(d) Evaluate the pair \((P,Q)\) at \(C_1C_0=10\), \(U=1\), \(V=0\) and at \(C_1C_0=11\), \(U=1\), \(V=0\).

(e) A stuck-at-1 fault on \(E_{\mathrm{VP}}\) occurs while \(C_1C_0=10\), \(U=1\), and \(V=0\). Determine \(P\) and \(Q\) under the bus-value rules, and name every buffer that is enabled.

DLOG:9918178.statement:
An 8-bit universal shifter operates on the word \(Q_7\ldots Q_0=1011\,0100\). The five one-bit operations below are defined with \(Q_7\) as the sign bit when arithmetic shifts are used:
- logical shift right, fill \(0\);
- arithmetic shift right, fill from \(Q_7\);
- rotate right;
- logical shift left, fill \(0\);
- rotate left.

(a) Compute the 8-bit result of each of the five operations applied once to \(1011\,0100\).
(b) Starting again from \(1011\,0100\), apply three successive arithmetic right shifts. Report the resulting word and the signed decimal values of both the original word and the result, each interpreted as 8-bit two’s complement.
(c) Give a sequence of identical rotate operations that swaps the two nibbles of \(1011\,0100\), and report the resulting word.
(d) A control unit must realise an arithmetic left shift of a two’s-complement byte (multiply by \(2\)) using only the logical-left-shift hardware. State the additional overflow check on \(Q_7\) and the post-shift \(Q_7\) that decides whether the result is representable, and apply it to \(1011\,0100\) and to \(0011\,0100\).

DLOG:9918198.statement:
A synchronous Moore machine receives a serial bit \(x\) and produces two outputs: \(p\), the even parity of all bits consumed since reset (the empty string has even parity), and \(s\), which equals \(1\) if and only if at least one consumed bit has been \(1\). Use the four states \(E_0\), \(Z\), \(E\), and \(O\) with the following meanings:

- \(E_0\) is the reset state, in which no bits have been consumed, so \(p=0\) and \(s=0\);
- \(Z\) is entered after a nonempty string consisting only of zeros, so \(p=0\) and \(s=0\);
- \(E\) is entered after a nonempty string of even parity that contains at least one \(1\), so \(p=0\) and \(s=1\);
- \(O\) is entered after a string of odd parity, so \(p=1\) and \(s=1\).

Every bit is consumed on the rising clock and updates the present prefix.

(a) Construct the Moore transition/output table on these four states, listing the pair \((p,s)\) for each state and the next state for \(x=0\) and for \(x=1\).
(b) Minimize the machine by partition refinement. Report the equivalence classes and a reduced table that retains a unique reset state.
(c) From reset, apply \(0,0,1,0,1,1\). Report the present state name in the unminimized four-state machine, the pair \((p,s)\), and the parity and \(s\)-value of the consumed prefix, after reset and after every clock.

DLOG:9918214.statement:
Four \(256\times 4\) ROM chips \(U_0,U_1,U_2,U_3\) are used to build one \(512\times 8\) ROM. Each chip has address pins \(p_7\ldots p_0\), data pins \(d_3\ldots d_0\) (\(d_3\) most significant), and an active-low chip select \(\overline{\mathrm{CS}}\). The system address is the 9-bit word \(A_8\ldots A_0\) and the system data bus is \(D_7\ldots D_0\) (\(D_7\) most significant). The intended organization is:

- \(A_7\ldots A_0\) drive \(p_7\ldots p_0\) of every chip;
- \(A_8=0\) selects the lower \(256\) system addresses and \(A_8=1\) selects the upper \(256\);
- \(U_1\) (when selected) drives \(D_7\ldots D_4\) and \(U_0\) drives \(D_3\ldots D_0\);
- \(U_3\) (when selected) drives \(D_7\ldots D_4\) and \(U_2\) drives \(D_3\ldots D_0\).

(a) Write active-low Boolean equations for \(\overline{\mathrm{CS}}_{U_0}\), \(\overline{\mathrm{CS}}_{U_1}\), \(\overline{\mathrm{CS}}_{U_2}\), and \(\overline{\mathrm{CS}}_{U_3}\) in terms of \(A_8\).

(b) For system address \(\mathrm{1A5}_{16}\), state which chips are selected and the \(8\)-bit internal address applied to those chips.

(c) Chip \(U_1\) stores nibble \(\mathrm{C}_{16}\) at internal address \(\mathrm{A5}_{16}\), and \(U_0\) stores nibble \(\mathrm{3}_{16}\) at the same internal address. Determine the system data byte at system address \(\mathrm{0A5}_{16}\).

DLOG:9918266.statement:
A one-hot FSM has five legal states \(S_0,\ldots,S_4\), encoded on five flip-flops \(Q_0,\ldots,Q_4\) with exactly one bit high in every legal state. Transitions:
\[
S_0\xrightarrow{X=0}S_0,\;
S_0\xrightarrow{X=1}S_1,\;
S_1\to S_2,\;
S_2\to S_3,\;
S_3\xrightarrow{Y=0}S_4,\;
S_3\xrightarrow{Y=1}S_0,\;
S_4\to S_0,
\]
where unmarked arrows occur unconditionally on the next clock. Next-state logic is the standard one-hot form \(D_j=\bigvee_{i:i\to j}(Q_i\land g_{ij})\) with hold of \(S_0\) via \(D_0=Q_0\overline{X}+Q_3 Y+Q_4\). Flip-flops have asynchronous reset into \(S_0\) (\(Q_0=1\), others \(0\)). Clock period \(T=4.00\,\mathrm{ns}\); combinational delays of the next-state ORs are in \([0.4,1.1]\,\mathrm{ns}\); \(t_{\mathrm{CQ,max}}=0.20\,\mathrm{ns}\), \(t_{\mathrm{su}}=0.10\,\mathrm{ns}\), \(t_{\mathrm{h}}=0.05\,\mathrm{ns}\); skew \(80\,\mathrm{ps}\).

(a) Confirm that every legal next-state vector is one-hot, and write the five excitation equations explicitly.

