DM:9919017.canonicalSolution:
Fix \(x\in\mathbb{R}\) with \(x\neq 1\). Let \(P(n)\) be the displayed identity, for integers \(n\ge 0\).

**Base case.** For \(n=0\) the left-hand side is \(x^0=1\). The right-hand side is \((x^{1}-1)/(x-1)=1\). Thus \(P(0)\) holds.

**Inductive hypothesis.** Fix \(n\ge 0\) and assume \(P(n)\).

**Inductive step.** Adding the next term \(x^{n+1}\) yields
\begin{align*}
\sum_{k=0}^{n+1}x^k
&=\sum_{k=0}^{n}x^k+x^{n+1}
=\frac{x^{n+1}-1}{x-1}+x^{n+1}\\
&=\frac{x^{n+1}-1+x^{n+1}(x-1)}{x-1}
=\frac{x^{n+1}-1+x^{n+2}-x^{n+1}}{x-1}
=\frac{x^{n+2}-1}{x-1},
\end{align*}
which is \(P(n+1)\). (The common denominator is lawful because \(x\neq 1\).)

By induction, \(P(n)\) holds for every integer \(n\ge 0\). Since \(x\neq 1\) was arbitrary, the identity holds for every such \(x\) and every \(n\ge 0\).

**Final answer.** \(\sum_{k=0}^{n}x^k=(x^{n+1}-1)/(x-1)\) for \(x\neq 1\), \(n\ge 0\).

