DM:9919124.canonicalSolution:
Let \(L_i\) be the event that the item comes from line \(i\), and let \(D\) be the event that it is defective. Then \(P(L_1)=\frac12\), \(P(L_2)=\frac{3}{10}\), \(P(L_3)=\frac15\), and \(P(D\mid L_1)=\frac{1}{50}\), \(P(D\mid L_2)=\frac{3}{100}\), \(P(D\mid L_3)=\frac{1}{20}\). The law of total probability gives
\begin{align*}
P(D)
&=P(D\mid L_1)P(L_1)+P(D\mid L_2)P(L_2)+P(D\mid L_3)P(L_3)\\
&=\frac12\cdot\frac{1}{50}+\frac{3}{10}\cdot\frac{3}{100}+\frac15\cdot\frac{1}{20}
=\frac{1}{100}+\frac{9}{1000}+\frac{1}{100}
=\frac{29}{1000}.
\end{align*}
Bayes' theorem then yields
\[
P(L_3\mid D)=\frac{P(D\mid L_3)P(L_3)}{P(D)}=\frac{(1/5)\cdot(1/20)}{29/1000}=\frac{1/100}{29/1000}=\frac{10}{29}.
\]

**Final answer.** \(P(D)=\dfrac{29}{1000}\); \(P(L_3\mid D)=\dfrac{10}{29}\)

