DM:9919140.canonicalSolution:
Let \(A_i\) be the event that die \(i\) shows a \(6\), for \(i=1,2,3\). The required probability is \(P(A_1\cup A_2\cup A_3)\). Independence and fairness give \(P(A_i)=\frac16\), \(P(A_i\cap A_j)=\frac{1}{36}\) for \(i\neq j\), and \(P(A_1\cap A_2\cap A_3)=\frac{1}{216}\). Inclusion-exclusion therefore yields
\begin{align*}
P(A_1\cup A_2\cup A_3)
&=\sum_i P(A_i)-\sum_{i<j}P(A_i\cap A_j)+P(A_1\cap A_2\cap A_3)\\
&=3\cdot\frac16-3\cdot\frac{1}{36}+\frac{1}{216}
=\frac12-\frac{1}{12}+\frac{1}{216}
=\frac{108-18+1}{216}
=\frac{91}{216}.
\end{align*}
(As a check, the complement is that no die shows a \(6\), which has probability \(\bigl(\frac56\bigr)^3=\frac{125}{216}\), and \(1-\frac{125}{216}=\frac{91}{216}\). The problem asked for inclusion-exclusion, which is the computation above.)

**Final answer.** \(\dfrac{91}{216}\)

