DM:9919193.canonicalSolution:
The graph \(G\) consists of the 4-cycle \(A{-}B{-}C{-}D{-}A\) with diagonal \(AC\), together with a vertex \(E\) adjacent only to \(B\) and to \(D\). Spanning trees have 4 edges. Classify them by the edges incident with \(E\).

Write \(H:=G-E\), so \(H\) is \(C_4\) plus the diagonal \(AC\). The eight spanning trees of \(H\) are
\[
\begin{align*}
&\{AB,BC,CD\},\quad
\{BC,CD,DA\},\quad
\{CD,DA,AB\},\quad
\{DA,AB,BC\},\\
&\{AC,AB,CD\},\quad
\{AC,AB,DA\},\quad
\{AC,BC,CD\},\quad
\{AC,BC,DA\}.
\end{align*}
\]
(The first four omit \(AC\) and are the spanning trees of the 4-cycle; the last four keep \(AC\) and two further edges that do not complete a triangle with it.)

If a spanning tree of \(G\) uses exactly one of \(BE,DE\), then \(E\) is a leaf and the remaining three edges form a spanning tree of \(H\). This gives \(2\cdot 8=16\) spanning trees:
\[
\begin{align*}
&\{BE,AB,BC,CD\},\;
\{BE,BC,CD,DA\},\;
\{BE,CD,DA,AB\},\;
\{BE,DA,AB,BC\},\\
&\{BE,AC,AB,CD\},\;
\{BE,AC,AB,DA\},\;
\{BE,AC,BC,CD\},\;
\{BE,AC,BC,DA\},\\
&\{DE,AB,BC,CD\},\;
\{DE,BC,CD,DA\},\;
\{DE,CD,DA,AB\},\;
\{DE,DA,AB,BC\},\\
&\{DE,AC,AB,CD\},\;
\{DE,AC,AB,DA\},\;
\{DE,AC,BC,CD\},\;
\{DE,AC,BC,DA\}.
\end{align*}
\]

If a spanning tree uses both \(BE\) and \(DE\), the remaining two edges must be chosen from \(\{AB,BC,CD,DA,AC\}\) so that \(B\) and \(D\) lie in different components of that 2-edge forest (otherwise \(B{-}E{-}D\) closes a cycle) — which, since any two edges on four vertices form a forest of two components, is equivalent to \(B\) and \(D\) not already being joined by a path of length 2. The forbidden pairs are \(\{AB,DA\}\) and \(\{BC,CD\}\); the other eight pairs work:
\[
\begin{align*}
&\{BE,DE,AB,BC\},\;
\{BE,DE,AB,CD\},\;
\{BE,DE,AB,AC\},\;
\{BE,DE,BC,DA\},\\
&\{BE,DE,BC,AC\},\;
\{BE,DE,CD,DA\},\;
\{BE,DE,CD,AC\},\;
\{BE,DE,DA,AC\}.
\end{align*}
\]
In all there are \(16+8=24\) spanning trees.

**Final answer.** 24 spanning trees, namely the 16 listed with exactly one of \(BE,DE\) and the 8 listed with both.

