DM:9919290.canonicalSolution:
Let \( P(n) \) be the assertion that \( b_n = 4^n + 3^n \). We prove \( P(n) \) for every integer \( n \ge 0 \) by strong induction on \( n \). Because the recurrence expresses \( b_n \) in terms of the two immediately preceding terms, two consecutive base cases are required.

**Base cases.** For \( n = 0 \),
\[
4^0 + 3^0 = 1 + 1 = 2 = b_0,
\]
so \( P(0) \) holds. For \( n = 1 \),
\[
4^1 + 3^1 = 4 + 3 = 7 = b_1,
\]
so \( P(1) \) holds.

**Inductive step.** Fix an integer \( k \ge 2 \), and assume that \( P(m) \) holds for every integer \( m \) with \( 0 \le m < k \) (the strong inductive hypothesis). In particular \( P(k-1) \) and \( P(k-2) \) both hold, since \( k-1 \ge 1 \) and \( k-2 \ge 0 \). Thus
\[
b_{k-1} = 4^{k-1} + 3^{k-1}, \qquad b_{k-2} = 4^{k-2} + 3^{k-2}.
\]
The recurrence, which is available because \( k \ge 2 \), then gives
\begin{align*}
b_k
&= 7 b_{k-1} - 12 b_{k-2} \\
&= 7\bigl(4^{k-1} + 3^{k-1}\bigr) - 12\bigl(4^{k-2} + 3^{k-2}\bigr) \\
&= 7\cdot 4^{k-1} - 12\cdot 4^{k-2} + 7\cdot 3^{k-1} - 12\cdot 3^{k-2}.
\end{align*}
Factor \( 4^{k-2} \) from the first pair of terms and \( 3^{k-2} \) from the second:
\begin{align*}
7\cdot 4^{k-1} - 12\cdot 4^{k-2}
&= 7\cdot 4\cdot 4^{k-2} - 12\cdot 4^{k-2}
= (28-12)\,4^{k-2}
= 16\cdot 4^{k-2}
= 4^2\cdot 4^{k-2}
= 4^k, \\[0.4em]
7\cdot 3^{k-1} - 12\cdot 3^{k-2}
&= 7\cdot 3\cdot 3^{k-2} - 12\cdot 3^{k-2}
= (21-12)\,3^{k-2}
= 9\cdot 3^{k-2}
= 3^2\cdot 3^{k-2}
= 3^k.
\end{align*}
Adding these identities yields \( b_k = 4^k + 3^k \), which is \( P(k) \).

By strong induction, \( P(n) \) holds for every integer \( n \ge 0 \).

(The same algebra shows that both \( (4^n)_{n\ge 0} \) and \( (3^n)_{n\ge 0} \) separately satisfy the homogeneous recurrence \( x_n = 7x_{n-1}-12x_{n-2} \), which is why their sum, once matched to the two initial values, is the unique solution. The argument above does not rely on uniqueness of solutions, only on the inductive verification.)

**Final answer.** \(b_n=4^n+3^n\) for every integer \(n\ge 0\).

