DS:9921049.statement:
Splice for doubly linked lists. Let \(L\) be a well-formed non-circular doubly linked list as in Problem 47, and let \(A\) and \(B\) be pointers to nodes in \(L\) such that \(A\) occurs at or before \(B\) in the forward direction (possibly \(A=B\)). Let \(M\) be a second well-formed list, disjoint in memory from \(L\) (possibly empty).

Design `SPLICE(L, A, B, M)` that replaces the inclusive segment of \(L\) from \(A\) to \(B\) by the entire contents of \(M\). After the call:

- \(L\) is well-formed and its sequence is the prefix of \(L\) before \(A\), followed by the former sequence of \(M\), followed by the suffix of \(L\) after \(B\);
- \(M\) is empty (`head = tail = NIL`);
- all affected `prev`/`next`/`head`/`tail` fields are updated;
- the operation performs \(O(1)\) pointer assignments, independent of the lengths of \(L\) and \(M\) and of the distance from \(A\) to \(B\).

Write the pseudocode, including cases where \(A\) is head, \(B\) is tail, \(M\) is empty, and the segment \(A..B\) is the whole of \(L\). You may assume \(A..B\) is a contiguous well-formed segment (\(A\) can reach \(B\) by following `next`, and the inverse along `prev`).

DS:9921106.canonicalSolution:
Because the tree is unaugmented, a fully-contained in-range subtree cannot be counted in \(O(1)\); its nodes must be visited. The algorithm therefore (i) walks a single path to the *split node* (the first node whose key lies in \([a,b]\), which is the unique highest in-range node if \(t \ge 1\)), (ii) from each in-range node on the two range boundaries, fully counts any child subtree that the BST property places entirely inside \([a,b]\), and (iii) steps inward along each boundary.

```
SIZE(u)                         // |T_u|; used only on subtrees known to lie in [a, b]
    if u = NIL then return 0
    return 1 + SIZE(u.left) + SIZE(u.right)

COUNT-L(u, a)                   // count keys ≥ a in T_u; every key of T_u is already ≤ b
    if u = NIL then return 0
    if u.key < a then
        return COUNT-L(u.right, a)
    else
        return 1 + SIZE(u.right) + COUNT-L(u.left, a)

COUNT-R(u, b)                   // count keys ≤ b in T_u; every key of T_u is already ≥ a
    if u = NIL then return 0
    if u.key > b then
        return COUNT-R(u.left, b)
    else
        return 1 + SIZE(u.left) + COUNT-R(u.right, b)

RC(u, a, b)
    if u = NIL then return 0
    if u.key < a then return RC(u.right, a, b)
    if u.key > b then return RC(u.left,  a, b)
    // a ≤ u.key ≤ b: u is the split node of this subtree
    return 1 + COUNT-L(u.left, a) + COUNT-R(u.right, b)

RANGE-COUNT(T, a, b)
    return RC(T.root, a, b)
```

**Correctness.** If \(u.\mathrm{key} < a\), the BST property discards \(T_{u.\mathrm{left}}\) (all still \(< a\)). If \(u.\mathrm{key} > b\), it discards \(T_{u.\mathrm{right}}\). If \(a \le u.\mathrm{key} \le b\), then \(u\) itself contributes \(1\); every key of \(T_{u.\mathrm{left}}\) is \(< u.\mathrm{key} \le b\), so the in-range keys there are exactly those \(\ge a\), which \(\mathrm{COUNT}\text{-}\mathrm{L}\) returns; every key of \(T_{u.\mathrm{right}}\) is \(> u.\mathrm{key} \ge a\), so the in-range keys there are exactly those \(\le b\), which \(\mathrm{COUNT}\text{-}\mathrm{R}\) returns. In \(\mathrm{COUNT}\text{-}\mathrm{L}\), a node \(u \ge a\) contributes itself, *all* of \(T_{u.\mathrm{right}}\) (keys \(> u.\mathrm{key} \ge a\) and already \(\le b\)), and the recursive count on the left. A node \(u < a\) can contribute only through its right subtree.

**Visit classification.** Let \(R\) be the (possibly empty) set of in-range nodes.

1. \(\mathrm{SIZE}\) is invoked only on a subtree that the BST property has already placed entirely inside \([a,b]\). Every node it visits is in \(R\).
2. The walk of \(\mathrm{RC}\) before the split node (if any) is a single downward path. If \(R \ne \emptyset\), that path is exactly the ancestor chain of the unique highest in-range node, hence consists of ancestors of a node in the range. If \(R = \emptyset\), the walk ends at a \(\mathrm{NIL}\) and never enters \(\mathrm{COUNT}\text{-}\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{COUNT}\text{-}\mathrm{R}\); only \(O(h)\) nodes on that vacant search path are visited, which the statement equivalently permits.
3. In \(\mathrm{COUNT}\text{-}\mathrm{L}\) (the \(\mathrm{COUNT}\text{-}\mathrm{R}\) argument is symmetric): a node with key \(\ge a\) is in \(R\). A node with key \(< a\) is visited only along the unique rightward search for the lower endpoint. Every such node that still has an in-range descendant is an ancestor of a node in the range; the *first* such node is the left child of an in-range node (the split node, or an in-range node on the left boundary), hence is a child of a node in the range.

The three-set claim of the statement therefore holds for every visited node that has an in-range descendant, together with every in-range node. After the last in-range key on a boundary, the algorithm may still examine a vacant rightward (resp. leftward) suffix of length at most \(h\) and the terminal \(\mathrm{NIL}\). Those vacant real nodes need not be children of a node in \(R\). They number at most \(O(h)\) in total (one suffix per boundary, plus the vacant root-to-nil path when \(t=0\)). The statement equivalently authorizes “only \(O(h)\) nodes outside the reported range,” and that budget is met: the nodes visited *outside* \(R\) lie on the root-to-split path plus two boundary paths, altogether \(O(h)\) nodes.

\(\mathrm{SIZE}\) is called on pairwise disjoint in-range subtrees, so it costs \(\Theta(t')\) where \(t' \le t\) is the number of nodes in those subtrees. Each of \(\mathrm{RC}\), \(\mathrm{COUNT}\text{-}\mathrm{L}\), and \(\mathrm{COUNT}\text{-}\mathrm{R}\) follows a single path of length at most \(h\), doing \(O(1)\) work plus a \(\mathrm{SIZE}\) call at in-range nodes on that path. Total time \(O(h + t)\). Extra memory \(O(h)\) for recursion (or \(O(1)\) if \(\mathrm{SIZE}\) and the boundary walks are written iteratively).

