DS:9921124.canonicalSolution:
**Construction.** The insertion order is the level-order of the complete BST on these eleven keys. No insertion rotates. The tree before any deletion is
\[
\begin{array}{c}
50(1)\\
\begin{array}{cc}
30(0)&70(0)\\
\begin{array}{cc}20(0)&40(0)\end{array}
&
\begin{array}{cc}60(0)&80(0)\end{array}\\
\begin{array}{cc}10(0)&25(0)\end{array}
\begin{array}{cc}35(0)&45(0)\end{array}
\end{array}
\end{array}
\]
Left height \(2\), right height \(1\).

**(a)** Delete \(10\). The node \(10\) is a leaf; splice it out. Parent \(20\) now has only the right child \(25\), so \(\mathrm{BF}(20)=-1\), but \(h(20)\) remains \(1\). No ancestor becomes unbalanced. No rotation. Tree: root \(50(1)\); left \(30(0)\) with \(20(-1)\) (right child \(25(0)\)) and \(40(0)\) (children \(35(0),45(0)\)); right \(70(0)\) with \(60(0),80(0)\).

Delete \(80\). Leaf; parent \(70\) now has only the left child \(60\), so \(\mathrm{BF}(70)=+1\), height still \(1\). No rotation. Same shape with \(80\) gone and \(70(1)\).

Delete \(30\). Node \(30\) has two children. Its inorder successor is \(35\) (leftmost node of the right subtree). Copy key \(35\) into the node that held \(30\), then physically delete the leaf that held \(35\). Parent \(40\) of that leaf now has only the right child \(45\), so \(\mathrm{BF}(40)=-1\); \(h(40)\) remains \(1\). The node now storing \(35\) still has left height \(1\) and right height \(1\). No rotation. Final tree:
\[
\begin{array}{c}
50(1)\\
\begin{array}{cc}
35(0)&70(1)\\
\begin{array}{cc}20(-1)&40(-1)\end{array}
&
\begin{array}{cc}60(0)&\end{array}\\
\begin{array}{cc} &25(0)\end{array}
\begin{array}{cc} &45(0)\end{array}
\end{array}
\end{array}
\]

**(b)** Walk from the parent of the physically removed node (\(40\)) to the root (\(50\)): the nodes visited are \(40\), then the node now storing \(35\), then \(50\). None of these three heights changes (\(1\), \(2\), and \(3\) respectively, both before and after). The list is empty.

**(c)** The final key set is \(\{20,25,35,40,45,50,60,70\}\). The tree above is not unique. One other AVL tree on the same keys is
\[
\begin{array}{c}
40(0)\\
\begin{array}{cc}
25(0)&60(0)\\
\begin{array}{cc}20(0)&35(0)\end{array}
&
\begin{array}{cc}50(1)&70(0)\end{array}\\
&&45(0)
\end{array}
\end{array}
\]
(every \(|\mathrm{BF}|\le 1\), and the root is \(40\) rather than \(50\)). In fact \(N(3)=7\le 8<12=N(4)\), so every AVL tree on eight nodes has height exactly \(3\), but many shapes exist.

Each deletion walks \(O(h)=O(1)\) nodes on this instance and performs zero rotations. After each deletion the tree remains a BST with \(|\mathrm{BF}|\le 1\), because every shortened node went from \(\mathrm{BF}=0\) to \(\mathrm{BF}=\pm 1\) without a height drop.

