DS:9921150.statement:
Design a single data structure that stores a dynamic set \(S\) of keys from a totally ordered universe and supports the following operations, all in \(O(\log n)\) worst-case time on \(n=|S|\):

Insert(\(k\)): add \(k\) to \(S\), assuming \(k\notin S\);

Delete(\(k\)): remove \(k\) from \(S\), assuming \(k\in S\);

Rank(\(k\)): return \(|\{x\in S:x<k\}|\), whether or not \(k\in S\);

Select(\(i\)): return the \(i\)-th smallest key of \(S\) (1-based), \(1\le i\le n\);

CountRange(\(a,b\)): return \(|\{x\in S:a\le x\le b\}|\);

Next(\(k\)): return the smallest key in \(S\) that is strictly greater than \(k\), or NIL if none exists.

You must use an AVL tree or a red-black tree as the underlying shape (choose one and keep it throughout). You may augment nodes with a constant number of integer fields.

(a) Specify the underlying balanced tree, every extra field stored at a node, and the exact \(O(1)\) updates of those fields during a rotation (give pseudocode for one left or right rotation with field updates).

(b) Write language-neutral pseudocode for all six operations. CountRange and Next must not call Select or Rank more than a constant number of times, unless you prove that such calls still yield a correct \(O(\log n)\) bound and do not hide linear work.

(c) Prove that each operation runs in \(O(\log n)\) time, using a height bound proved in this batch (cite the statement number and the bound you use).

(d) Prove that the extra fields remain correct after every Insert and Delete, by an invariant that you state and maintain through BST restructuring and through rotations.

(e) Briefly argue that CountRange cannot be supported in \(o(\log n)\) worst-case time in the comparison model on a linear-size structure, by reduction from Search or from the lower bound of Problem 147(d).

DS:9921163.statement:
Design a dynamic chaining hash table that stores \(n\) distinct keys and whose table size \(m\) is always a power of \(2\), with \(m\ge 1\). Maintain the load-factor invariant: if \(n=0\) then \(m=1\) and the unique slot is empty; if \(n\ge 1\) then \(1/4\le n/m\le 1\). Hashing a key and a pointer update are \(O(1)\). Rehashing all keys into a new table of size \(m'\) costs \(\Theta(n+m+m')\) time.

Specify:
  (i) the exact conditions under which \(\mathrm{INSERT}\) grows the table, and to what new size;
  (ii) the exact conditions under which \(\mathrm{DELETE}\) shrinks the table, and to what new size;
  (iii) pseudocode for \(\mathrm{INSERT}(T,k)\) and \(\mathrm{DELETE}(T,k)\) including rehashing, assuming the static operations of Problem 153 on a table of fixed size.
Then prove that in any sequence of \(s\) operations (inserts and deletes of keys, starting from an empty table with \(m=1\)) the total time spent in rehashing is \(O(s)\). You may use an accounting argument or a potential function; if you use a potential, define \(\Phi\) explicitly and show \(\Phi\ge 0\) after every operation.

DS:9921271.statement:
Design a single tree-based dictionary that stores distinct comparable keys, each paired with a numeric value. Support the following operations in \(O(\log n)\) worst-case time on \(n\) stored keys, using only standard binary-search-tree primitives plus constant-size subtree aggregates:

- \(\mathrm{INSERT}(k, v)\): insert key \(k\) with value \(v\), or replace the value if \(k\) is already present;
- \(\mathrm{DELETE}(k)\): remove key \(k\) if present;
- \(\mathrm{RANK}(k)\): return the number of stored keys strictly less than \(k\);
- \(\mathrm{SELECT}(i)\): return the key of rank \(i\) (0-based), or \(\mathrm{NIL}\) if \(i\) is out of range;
- \(\mathrm{SUMRANGE}(L, R)\): return the sum of values of all stored keys \(k\) with \(L\le k\le R\) (empty range sums to \(0\)).

Specify every field stored in a node, state the exact invariants relating those fields to the stored keys and values, and give language-neutral pseudocode for \(\mathrm{SELECT}\) and \(\mathrm{SUMRANGE}\) that maintains the invariants. Argue, from the invariants, that both procedures are correct and run in \(O(\mathrm{height})\) time. You may assume an underlying balanced BST (AVL or red-black) whose rotations you may call; show how each rotation restores the aggregates.

DS:9921283.canonicalSolution:
**Canonical decomposition.** In a balanced BST on \(x\)-coordinates, the set of points whose \(x\)-coordinate lies in \([x_1,x_2]\) is the disjoint union of the point-sets of \(O(\log n)\) nodes of \(T_x\).

A size-augmented BST supports 1-dimensional range count in \(O(\log n)\):

```
RANK_Y(u, z):                       // # of points in this Ty-tree with y < z
    c ← 0
    while u ≠ NIL:
        if u.p.y ≥ z: u ← left(u)
        else: c ← c + size(left(u)) + 1; u ← right(u)
    return c

YCOUNT(v, y1, y2) = RANK_Y(root(Ty(v)), y2+ε) - RANK_Y(root(Ty(v)), y1)
                  // (# with y ≤ y2) − (# with y < y1)
```

(The keys have distinct \(y\)-coordinates, so \(y_2+\varepsilon\) means “strictly greater than \(y_2\)”; equivalently use a companion \(\mathrm{RANKLEQ}\).)

The set \(C\) of canonical \(x\)-nodes is computed from the split node (the first node of \(T_x\) whose point lies in \([x_1,x_2]\)):

```
CANONICAL-LEFT-OF-SPLIT(u, x1):     // u is in the left subtree of the split
    C ← ∅
    while u ≠ NIL:
        if u.p.x ≥ x1:
            if right(u) ≠ NIL: C ← C ∪ {right(u)}
