DS:9921291.statement:
A disjoint-set structure on \(\{1,2,\ldots,n\}\) stores an integer weight \(w[i]\) on each element. In addition to \(\mathrm{UNION}(x,y)\) (unite the sets containing \(x\) and \(y\); if already united, do nothing) and \(\mathrm{FIND}(x)\), support

- \(\mathrm{SUM}(x)\): return the sum of weights of all elements in the current set of \(x\);
- \(\mathrm{ADD}(x,\delta)\): add \(\delta\) to \(w[x]\) (equivalently, to the set sum of \(x\)’s set, but the weight remains attached to element \(x\));
- \(\mathrm{SNAPSHOT}\): push a marker onto an undo log;
- \(\mathrm{ROLLBACK}\): undo every UNION and ADD performed since the most recent unmatched SNAPSHOT, restoring the partition and all weights; if there is no unmatched SNAPSHOT, restore the initial singletons and original weights.

Implement UNION by linking by rank without path compression, so that FIND is not destructive. Implement ADD so that SUM remains correct without walking an entire component. Use an explicit undo log of constant-size records.

State every field stored at a node and the invariants that make \(\mathrm{SUM}(x)\) correct after any sequence of UNION and ADD. Give language-neutral pseudocode for UNION, ADD, SUM, SNAPSHOT, and ROLLBACK. Prove that ROLLBACK restores both the partition and the weights that existed at the corresponding SNAPSHOT, and that a sequence of \(m\) operations runs in \(O(m\log n)\) time. Explain why path compression would make ROLLBACK asymptotically harder if the undo log may store only \(O(1)\) words per UNION.

NUM:9922004.statement:
