NUM:9922149.canonicalSolution:
The interpolant is the unique linear polynomial through \((0,0)\) and \((1,1)\), namely \(P_1(x)=x\). The standard error formula would require \(f\in C^2[0,1]\). But
\[
f'(x)=\frac{1}{2\sqrt{x}},\qquad
f''(x)=-\frac{1}{4}x^{-3/2}
\qquad(x>0),
\]
so \(f'\) (and a fortiori \(f''\)) is unbounded as \(x\to 0^+\). Thus \(f\notin C^1[0,1]\) and \(f\notin C^2[0,1]\), and the hypotheses of the error formula fail.

For the quotient itself, with \(x\in(0,1)\),
\begin{align*}
\frac{f(x)-P_1(x)}{x(x-1)}
&=\frac{\sqrt{x}-x}{x(x-1)}
=\frac{\sqrt{x}\,(1-\sqrt{x})}{x\,(\sqrt{x}-1)(\sqrt{x}+1)}
=\frac{\sqrt{x}}{x\bigl(-(\sqrt{x}+1)\bigr)}
=-\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}+x},
\end{align*}
where the cancellation of the factor \(1-\sqrt{x}=-(\sqrt{x}-1)\) is valid for \(x\neq 1\). As \(x\to 0^+\),
\[
-\frac{1}{\sqrt{x}+x}\to-\infty.
\]
The limit is not a finite real number. (This is the quantitative manifestation of \(f''\) blowing up at the origin: the error formula would have identified the quotient with \(f''(\xi)/2\), which cannot stay bounded.)

