NUM:9922247.canonicalSolution:
The nodes \(\{0,2/3,4/3,2\}\,\mathrm{m}\) are equally spaced with mesh width \(h=2/3\,\mathrm{m}\). The complete-spline bound therefore gives
\begin{align*}
\|g-s\|_\infty
&\le\frac{5}{384}\Bigl(\frac{2}{3}\Bigr)^4\cdot 48.0
=\frac{5}{384}\cdot\frac{16}{81}\cdot 48
=\frac{5\cdot 16\cdot 48}{384\cdot 81}
=\frac{5\cdot 16}{8\cdot 81}
=\frac{10}{81}\,\mathrm{m}
\approx 0.12346\,\mathrm{m}.
\end{align*}

The complete-cubic estimate uses the Hermite end conditions \(s'=g'\) at both endpoints. Those conditions make the end defects in the first derivative vanish, and the Peano/Green’s-function argument then produces an \(O(h^4)\) bound involving only \(g^{(4)}\). Natural end conditions instead impose \(s''=0\) at the ends. If \(g''(0)\) and \(g''(2)\) are not both zero, then \(s''-g''\) fails to vanish at an endpoint, so the spline does not match the Hermite data that the \(5/384\) theorem requires. The mismatch in second derivative is \(O(1)\) as \(h\to 0\), and a standard Taylor comparison on the first panel then yields an interpolation error of order \(O(h^2)\) near that endpoint. Interior panels can still be more accurate, but the global max-norm is limited by the boundary. The typical reduced endpoint order for natural cubics, when \(g''(\mathrm{ends})\ne 0\), is therefore \(2\). One cannot invoke the displayed \(O(h^4)\) bound.

