(b) \(01111+00001=10000\), carry-out \(0\). Integers \(15+1=16\notin\{-16,\ldots,15\}\). The bit string \(10000\) reads as \(-16\). Signed overflow. Flags: \((0,1)\).

(c) \(11111+11111=11110\) with carry-out \(1\). Integers \((-1)+(-1)=-2\), matching \(S\). No signed overflow. Flags: \((1,0)\).

(d) \(10000+10000=00000\) with carry-out \(1\). Integers \((-16)+(-16)=-32\notin\{-16,\ldots,15\}\). The bit string \(00000\) reads as \(0\). Signed overflow. Flags: \((1,1)\).

The four pairs realize \((0,0)\), \((0,1)\), \((1,0)\), and \((1,1)\) respectively. No combination is missing.

**Answers.**
(a) \(S=01000\), cout \(0\), no OF;
(b) \(S=10000\), cout \(0\), OF;
(c) \(S=11110\), cout \(1\), no OF;
(d) \(S=00000\), cout \(1\), OF;
none of the four flag combinations is missing.

ARCH:9923063.canonicalSolution:
A load-word instruction writes the register file at the capturing clock edge. The PC is an edge-triggered register, so its output (the fetch address) is valid only after clock-to-Q. The stated path, with that sequential overhead, is

\[
\begin{align*}
T_{\mathrm{LW}}
&= t_{\mathrm{CLK\text{-}to-}Q}
+ t_{\mathrm{IM}}
+ t_{\mathrm{RF,read}}
+ t_{\mathrm{MUX,ALUSrc}}
+ t_{\mathrm{ALU}}
+ t_{\mathrm{DM}}
+ t_{\mathrm{MUX,MemtoReg}}
+ t_{\mathrm{SU,RF}}\\
&= 30+200+150+25+180+220+25+20\\
&= 850\,\mathrm{ps}.
\end{align*}
\]

Terms included, in order: PC clock-to-Q (30 ps), instruction memory (200 ps), register-file read (150 ps), ALUSrc mux (25 ps), ALU (180 ps), data memory (220 ps), MemtoReg mux (25 ps), register-file write setup (20 ps).

Terms excluded, with reasons:

- Sign extender (15 ps) and control (70 ps) both start after the instruction is available and finish before the 150 ps register-file read, so neither sits on this path. The ALUSrc select and the sign-extended immediate therefore arrive at the ALUSrc mux before ReadData2.
- Shift-left-2, the 32-bit adder, the AND gate, and PC setup are used only by next-PC logic, not by the load’s register-file write path.
- Wiring delay is given as zero.

Thus the minimum clock period that correctly executes load-word is \(850\,\mathrm{ps}\).

