ELEC:9924065.canonicalSolution:
**(a)** \(v_{OV}=1.90-0.70=1.20\,\mathrm{V}\). The saturation formula applies for \(v_{DS}\ge v_{OV}\), so the smallest such drain-source voltage is \(1.20\,\mathrm{V}\).

**(b)** Both \(2.00\,\mathrm{V}\) and \(6.00\,\mathrm{V}\) exceed \(1.20\,\mathrm{V}\), so both points are in saturation. The prefactor is
\[
\tfrac12 k_n v_{OV}^2=\tfrac12(1.20\,\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{V}^2)(1.20\,\mathrm{V})^2=0.864\,\mathrm{mA},
\]
hence
\[
i_D(2.00\,\mathrm{V})=0.864(1+0.040\cdot 2.00)=0.933\,\mathrm{mA},
\]
\[
i_D(6.00\,\mathrm{V})=0.864(1+0.040\cdot 6.00)=1.07\,\mathrm{mA}.
\]
(More digits: \(0.93312\,\mathrm{mA}\) and \(1.07136\,\mathrm{mA}\).)

**(c)** Chord slope:
\[
\frac{1.07136-0.93312}{4.00}=0.03456\,\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{V}=34.6\,\mu\mathrm{A}/\mathrm{V}.
\]

**(d)** Differentiating the saturation formula at fixed \(v_{GS}\),
\[
\frac{\partial i_D}{\partial v_{DS}}=\tfrac12 k_n v_{OV}^2\lambda=0.864\cdot 0.040=0.03456\,\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{V}=34.6\,\mu\mathrm{A}/\mathrm{V},
\]
independent of the evaluation point \(v_{DS}=2.00\,\mathrm{V}\).

**(e)** Part (c) is the slope of the secant joining the two marked points on the \(i_D\)--\(v_{DS}\) curve; part (d) is the slope of the tangent at \(v_{DS}=2.00\,\mathrm{V}\). They do **not** differ: the saturation law is affine in \(v_{DS}\), so every secant of the saturation branch coincides with the tangent. (The request to explain a difference is therefore vacuous for this model.)

\[\boxed{(a)\ v_{OV}=1.20\,\mathrm{V},\ v_{DS,\min}=1.20\,\mathrm{V}
} \]
\[\boxed{(b)\ i_D(2\,\mathrm{V})=0.933\,\mathrm{mA},\ i_D(6\,\mathrm{V})=1.07\,\mathrm{mA}
} \]
\[\boxed{(c)\ 34.6\,\mu\mathrm{A}/\mathrm{V}
} \]
\[\boxed{(d)\ 34.6\,\mu\mathrm{A}/\mathrm{V}
} \]
\[\boxed{(e)\ \text{secant vs tangent; equal because }i_D\text{ is affine in }v_{DS}
} \]

