ELEC:9924068.canonicalSolution:
**(a)** The mistaken test is the NMOS cutoff rule \(v_{GS}<V_t\) applied verbatim to a PMOS. Correct algebraic cutoff: \(v_{GS}>V_{tp}\). Correct source-referenced cutoff: \(v_{SG}<|V_{tp}|\).

**(b)** \(v_{SG}=4.80-3.10=1.70\,\mathrm{V}\ge 0.75\,\mathrm{V}\) and \(v_{SD}=4.80-2.40=2.40\,\mathrm{V}\), with overdrive \(1.70-0.75=0.95\,\mathrm{V}\). Then \(2.40\,\mathrm{V}\ge 0.95\,\mathrm{V}\), so **saturation**. Algebraically: \(v_{GS}=-1.70\,\mathrm{V}\le -0.75\,\mathrm{V}\) and \(v_{GD}=3.10-2.40=0.70\,\mathrm{V}\ge -0.75\,\mathrm{V}\), same conclusion.

**(c)**
\[
i_{SD}=\tfrac12(0.40\,\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{V}^2)(0.95\,\mathrm{V})^2=0.181\,\mathrm{mA}.
\]

**(d)** An enhancement NMOS inverts when \(v_{GS}\) lies *above* a positive threshold; an enhancement PMOS inverts when \(v_{GS}\) lies *below* a negative threshold, so the inequality direction (or equivalently the use of \(v_{SG}\) versus \(|V_{tp}|\)) must reverse with the carrier type.

\[\boxed{(a)\ \text{mistaken }v_{GS}<V_t;\ \text{correct }v_{GS}>V_{tp}\ \text{or }v_{SG}<|V_{tp}|
} \]
\[\boxed{(b)\ \text{saturation}
} \]
\[\boxed{(c)\ i_{SD}=0.181\,\mathrm{mA}
} \]
\[\boxed{(d)\ \text{PMOS inversion is }v_{GS}\le V_{tp}<0,\ \text{not }v_{GS}<V_t
} \]

