ELEC:9924087.canonicalSolution:
Write \(v_{OV}=v_{GS}-V_{tn}\) when inverted, and define
\[
f(v):=i_D(v_{GS},v)-\frac{V_{DD}-v}{R_D},\qquad v\in[0,V_{DD}].
\]
An operating point is a root of \(f\). The load term is continuous and strictly decreasing in \(v\).

**(a)** If \(v_{GS}<V_{tn}\), then \(i_D\equiv 0\) for every \(v_{DS}\). The load line meets \(i=0\) at exactly one point of \([0,V_{DD}]\), namely \(v_{DS}=V_{DD}\), \(i=0\). That is the unique operating point (cutoff).

**(b)** \(\lambda=0\), \(v_{GS}>V_{tn}\). Continuity of \(i_D(\,\cdot\,)\) at pinch-off is Problem 62(a). On the open triode interval the derivative \(k_n(v_{OV}-v_{DS})\) is strictly positive, so \(i_D\) is strictly increasing there. On saturation, \(i_D\) is the constant \(\tfrac12 k_n v_{OV}^2\). Thus \(i_D\) is continuous and nondecreasing on \([0,\infty)\). Therefore \(f\) is continuous and *strictly* increasing on \([0,V_{DD}]\) (device nondecreasing, minus load strictly decreasing). Now \(f(0)=0-V_{DD}/R_D<0\) and \(f(V_{DD})=i_D(V_{DD})-0\ge 0\), so \(f\) has exactly one zero in \((0,V_{DD}]\).

**(c)** The property that changes is constancy on saturation: the \(\lambda>0\) saturation branch has strictly positive slope \(\tfrac12 k_n v_{OV}^2\lambda>0\), so \(i_D\) is strictly increasing on the whole half-line \(v_{DS}>0\) *except possibly at the pinch-off abscissa*. As written (triode branch unchanged), \(i_D\) jumps upward at \(v_{DS}=v_{OV}\) by \(\tfrac12 k_n\lambda v_{OV}^3\) (Problem 62(d)). On each connected piece (triode, then saturation) \(f\) remains strictly increasing. Hence \(f\) has at most one root on the triode piece and at most one root on the saturation piece, and it cannot have a root on both, because after the jump \(f\) is larger than its triode-side values. Thus there is **at most one** operating point. (Existence can fail if the load line threads the jump, i.e. if \(f(v_{OV}-)<0<f(v_{OV}+)\); if a \(C^0\) matching is imposed by multiplying the triode branch by \(1+\lambda v_{DS}\), then \(f\) is continuous and strictly increasing with \(f(0)<0\le f(V_{DD})\), and existence is restored as well.) Uniqueness, which is what was asked, is not lost.

**(d)** In a CMOS inverter the KCL residual at the output is \(i_{Dn}(v_{\mathrm{in}},v_{\mathrm{out}})-i_{SDp}(v_{\mathrm{in}},v_{\mathrm{out}})\), the difference of two device currents that are each nondecreasing in their own \(v_{DS}\) (or \(v_{SD}\)). That difference need not be strictly monotone in \(v_{\mathrm{out}}\) in every joint-region assignment, and with \(\lambda=0\) it is *independent* of \(v_{\mathrm{out}}\) throughout the both-saturation window. A strictly decreasing passive load line is what made \(f\) strictly increasing in (b)–(c); that ingredient is absent, so uniqueness must be argued separately (and can fail at the matched \(\lambda=0\) midpoint; cf. Problem 90).

\[\boxed{(a)\ \text{unique OP }(v_{DS},i)=(V_{DD},0)
} \]
\[\boxed{(b)\ i_D\text{ continuous, nondecreasing, strict on triode, flat on sat};\ f\text{ strictly increasing with opposite endpoint signs}
} \]
\[\boxed{(c)\ \text{sat branch strictly increasing (no longer flat)};\ \text{at most one OP remains}
} \]
\[\boxed{(d)\ \text{CMOS residual is a difference of two device currents, not a MOSFET vs a strictly decreasing resistor load}
} \]

