} \]

ELEC:9924090.statement:
Honors. A complementary MOS inverter is driven from \(V_{DD}=2.50\,\mathrm{V}\). The NMOS and PMOS are matched in the following sense: \(V_{tn}=|V_{tp}|=0.50\,\mathrm{V}\) and \(k_n=k_p=k>0\). Bodies are tied to the respective sources (NMOS source at \(0\), PMOS source at \(V_{DD}\)). Channel-length modulation is omitted. Gate currents are zero. The \(\lambda=0\) NMOS square-law (current \(i_{Dn}\) entering the NMOS drain) is: cutoff with \(i_{Dn}=0\) if \(v_{GSn}<V_{tn}\); triode \(i_{Dn}=k[(v_{GSn}-V_{tn})v_{DSn}-v_{DSn}^2/2]\) if \(v_{GSn}\ge V_{tn}\) and \(0\le v_{DSn}<v_{GSn}-V_{tn}\); saturation \(i_{Dn}=\frac12 k(v_{GSn}-V_{tn})^2\) if \(v_{GSn}\ge V_{tn}\) and \(v_{DSn}\ge v_{GSn}-V_{tn}\). The \(\lambda=0\) PMOS square-law (current \(i_{SDp}\) entering the PMOS source and leaving its drain) is: cutoff with \(i_{SDp}=0\) if \(v_{SGp}<|V_{tp}|\); triode \(i_{SDp}=k[(v_{SGp}-|V_{tp}|)v_{SDp}-v_{SDp}^2/2]\) if \(v_{SGp}\ge |V_{tp}|\) and \(0\le v_{SDp}<v_{SGp}-|V_{tp}|\); saturation \(i_{SDp}=\frac12 k(v_{SGp}-|V_{tp}|)^2\) if \(v_{SGp}\ge |V_{tp}|\) and \(v_{SDp}\ge v_{SGp}-|V_{tp}|\). Here \(v_{GSn}=v_{\mathrm{in}}\), \(v_{DSn}=v_{\mathrm{out}}\), \(v_{SGp}=V_{DD}-v_{\mathrm{in}}\), \(v_{SDp}=V_{DD}-v_{\mathrm{out}}\), and \(V_{tn}=|V_{tp}|=0.50\,\mathrm{V}\). The input \(v_{\mathrm{in}}\) is swept over \([0,V_{DD}]\); the output \(v_{\mathrm{out}}\) is the unique voltage at which \(i_{Dn}=i_{SDp}\) (uniqueness of \(v_{\mathrm{out}}\) at each \(v_{\mathrm{in}}\) may be used as a fact in this problem, but the region assignment must still be derived).

(a) Derive the five input intervals, including the treatment of endpoints, that correspond to the following joint assignments:
- \(M_n\) cutoff and \(M_p\) triode;
- \(M_n\) saturation and \(M_p\) triode;
- both saturation;
- \(M_n\) triode and \(M_p\) saturation;
- \(M_p\) cutoff and \(M_n\) triode.

The derivation must use the cutoff and pinch-off tests of both devices, not a memorized VTC sketch. For the both-saturation assignment, show that current equality together with \(k_n=k_p\) and \(V_{tn}=|V_{tp}|\) forces a single input, and determine the corresponding \(v_{\mathrm{out}}\).

(b) In the interval where \(M_n\) is in saturation and \(M_p\) is in triode, write the algebraic equation that determines \(v_{\mathrm{out}}\) as a function of \(v_{\mathrm{in}}\). You need not solve it in closed form in this part.

(c) Evaluate \(v_{\mathrm{out}}\) at \(v_{\mathrm{in}}=0.80\,\mathrm{V}\) by solving the equation of part (b), discarding any root that violates the joint region assignment, and reporting the surviving \(v_{\mathrm{out}}\).

(d) State, in two sentences, which conclusions of this piecewise-square-law VTC would change if \(\lambda>0\) were restored to both devices, and which conclusions (interval endpoints determined solely by thresholds) would not.

ELEC:9924154.statement:
An NMOS common-source voltage amplifier is to be designed for midband use with \(V_{DD}=5.00\,\mathrm{V}\). The source is at DC and AC ground. A single drain resistor \(R_D\) is the only load (\(R_L=\infty\)). Device parameters: \(V_{TN}=0.500\,\mathrm{V}\), \(k_n'=200\,\mu\mathrm{A/V^2}\), \(\lambda=0\). Use saturation square-law \(I_D=\frac12 k_n'(W/L)V_{OV}^2\) with \(V_{OV}=V_{GS}-V_{TN}>0\) and \(V_{DS}\ge V_{OV}\), and \(g_m=k_n'(W/L)V_{OV}=2I_D/V_{OV}\). The gate is driven by an ideal voltage source (zero source resistance). Allowed discrete choices: \(V_{OV}\in\{0.250,0.400,0.600\}\,\mathrm{V}\) and \(R_D\in\{2.00,3.00,4.70,6.80\}\,\mathrm{k}\Omega\). For each surviving pair the drain current is then fixed by the gain specification below, and \(W/L\) is fixed by the square law. Design constraints, all mandatory:
- loaded midband gain \(A_v=v_o/v_{gs}=-10.0\) exactly under \(\lambda=0\);
- available positive peak swing at the drain, \(I_D R_D\), at least \(1.00\,\mathrm{V}\) (onset of cutoff);
- available negative peak swing, \(V_D-V_{OV}\) with \(V_D=V_{DD}-I_D R_D\), at least \(1.00\,\mathrm{V}\) (onset of triode);
- \(0.300\,\mathrm{mA}\le I_D\le 2.00\,\mathrm{mA}\);
- \(W/L\le 40.0\).

(a) For every pair \((V_{OV},R_D)\) in the allowed lists, compute the \(I_D\) required by \(A_v=-10.0\), the corresponding \(W/L\), \(V_D\), both available peak swings, and a pass/fail mark against every constraint.
(b) List every pair that satisfies all constraints. If more than one pair survives, identify the survivor with the smallest \(I_D\) (lowest static power \(V_{DD}I_D\)).
(c) For the lowest-power surviving design, compute the static power and the largest sinusoidal \(|v_{gs}|\) for which both swing bounds remain satisfied in the linear square-law-plus-resistor model.
(d) Show that the pair \(V_{OV}=0.250\,\mathrm{V}\), \(R_D=6.80\,\mathrm{k}\Omega\) either survives or fails, and name every violated constraint in the failing case. Do not invent a device law other than the stated square law.

ELEC:9924159.canonicalSolution:
