The application’s Thread-mode stack region is \([\mathtt{0x20001800},\mathtt{0x20001FFF}]\), growing down. Handler mode continues to use MSP. The bootloader’s remaining MSP unused depth at the instant of the \(\mathrm{BX}\) is \(200\) bytes above \(\mathtt{0x20000000}\). An SVC instruction in the application’s first \(10\) instructions is taken from Thread mode: 8-word hardware frame on PSP, then Handler mode on MSP. The SVC handler uses \(96\) bytes of software frame on MSP. Ignore FPU and unprivileged mode.

(a) Report VTOR, PSP, the execution address of the first application instruction, and whether that address is 2-aligned. Decide whether PSP names a byte inside the application stack region.

(b) After the SVC hardware frame is pushed, report PSP. Compute remaining unused application-stack bytes.

(c) Compute MSP after the SVC software frame is allocated, using the bootloader’s remaining depth of \(200\) bytes as the unused MSP space at the \(\mathrm{BX}\) (so the SVC handler starts with those \(200\) bytes). Decide whether MSP overflows the region \([\mathtt{0x20000000},\mathtt{0x200007FF}]\).

