EMB:9925294.statement:
Write-protect hardware on Anvil-M4 Flash implements two independent locks, each sticky until reset:

- \(\mathrm{WRP0}\) covers \([\mathtt{0x08000000},\mathtt{0x0800FFFF}]\) (EmberBoot);
- \(\mathrm{WRP1}\) covers \([\mathtt{0x08010000},\mathtt{0x0801FFFF}]\) (the first \(64\,\mathrm{KiB}\) of slot \(A\)).

An erase or program to a protected byte is a no-op and sets flash error flag PGSERR. EmberBoot sets \(\mathrm{WRP0}=1\) before jumping to an application and never sets \(\mathrm{WRP1}\). Slot \(A\) base is \(\mathtt{0x08010000}\), slot \(B\) base is \(\mathtt{0x08030000}\). The application, running from slot \(A\), attempts the following operations in order; each is issued even if a previous one set PGSERR. PGSERR is sticky until software writes the clear bit.

1. erase the page at \(\mathtt{0x08000000}\);
2. program \(8\) bytes at \(\mathtt{0x08010010}\);
3. erase the page at \(\mathtt{0x08030000}\);
4. program \(8\) bytes at \(\mathtt{0x0800FFF8}\);
5. erase the page at \(\mathtt{0x0801F800}\).

(a) For each of the five operations, state whether the erase or program commits, and whether PGSERR is \(1\) after that operation.

(b) Determine whether EmberBoot can be overwritten by this application run. Determine whether the currently executing slot \(A\) page at \(\mathtt{0x08010000}\) can be erased by operation 2’s address (operation 2 is a program, not an erase).

(c) If \(\mathrm{WRP1}\) had also been set by EmberBoot, repeat (a) for operations 2 and 5 only.

(d) A field update stores a new bootloader image at \(\mathtt{0x08000000}\) using the application’s Flash driver. Using only WRP0, decide whether that update can succeed without a reset that clears WRP0. Audit the claim that “write-protect makes bricking impossible,” given that WRP1 is not set and slot \(A\) contains the only confirmed image.

SIG:9926043.canonicalSolution:
**Assumption check.** The difference of steps \(u[\cdot+1]-u[\cdot-2]\) is the indicator of the integer interval from \(-1\) through \(1\) inclusive, because \(u[n+1]\) turns on at \(n=-1\) and \(u[n-2]\) turns on at \(n=2\). Sifting gives \(x*h=3S_{-2}h-h+2S_3 h\). Finite supports make the sum identity \(\sum(x*h)=(\sum x)(\sum h)\) exact.

Nonzero samples of \(h\): \(h[-1]=h[0]=h[1]=1\), and \(h[n]=0\) otherwise. Therefore
\[
y[n]=3h[n+2]-h[n]+2h[n-3].
\]
Evaluating on the requested window:
\[
\begin{array}{c|ccccccccc}
n & -3 & -2 & -1 & 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5\\\hline
y[n] & 3 & 3 & 2 & -1 & -1 & 2 & 2 & 2 & 0
\end{array}
\]
The three shifted copies of \(h\) occupy \(\{-3,-2,-1\}\), \(\{-1,0,1\}\), and \(\{2,3,4\}\) respectively, so \(y[n]=0\) outside \(\{-3,\ldots,4\}\). (The sample at \(n=5\) is already \(0\).)

The kernel has \(h[-1]\neq 0\), so the system is not causal.

Sum of the tabulated nonzero values: \(3+3+2-1-1+2+2+2=12\). Independently, \(\sum x=3-1+2=4\) and \(\sum h=3\), so \((\sum x)(\sum h)=12\).

\[\boxed{h[-1]=h[0]=h[1]=1\text{ (else }0\text{)};
y=3h[\cdot+2]-h+2h[\cdot-3];
y[-3..5]=(3,3,2,-1,-1,2,2,2,0),\ \operatorname{supp}y\subseteq\{-3,\ldots,4\};
\mathrm{not\ causal};
\sum y=12=(\sum x)(\sum h)}\]

