CTRL:9927037.canonicalSolution:
**Stability check.** Every coefficient of each quadratic is positive, so the Routh–Hurwitz criterion places both poles of each \(T\) in the open LHP. DC gains therefore exist and equal \(1\).

**(a)–(b).**

- \(T_A\): \(\omega_n=\sqrt{196}=14.0\,\mathrm{rad/s}\), \(2\zeta\omega_n=5.60\) so \(\zeta=5.60/28.0=0.200\). Since \(\zeta<1\), the free response is **underdamped**. \(\sigma=2.80\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\), \(\omega_d=14.0\sqrt{1-0.040}=13.72\,\mathrm{rad/s}\). Poles: \(-2.80\pm j 13.72\).
- \(T_B\): \(\omega_n=\sqrt{48.0}=4\sqrt{3}=6.928\,\mathrm{rad/s}\), \(2\zeta\omega_n=16.0\) so \(\zeta=8/\sqrt{48}=2/\sqrt{3}=1.155>1\): **overdamped**. Discriminant \(16^{2}-4\cdot 48=64\), poles \((-16\pm 8)/2\), i.e. \(-4.00\) and \(-12.0\) (equivalently \(\tau=1/4\) and \(1/12\)).
- \(T_C\): \(\omega_n=\sqrt{25.0}=5.00\,\mathrm{rad/s}\), \(2\zeta\omega_n=10.0\) so \(\zeta=1\): **critically damped**. Repeated pole \(-5.00,-5.00\).

**(c).** Each DC gain is \(T(0)=1\), so \(\lim_{t\to\infty}y(t)=1.00\,\mathrm{V}\) for the unit step. A zero-free prototype overshoots its final value if and only if it is underdamped:
- \(T_A\) (underdamped): **can overshoot**;
- \(T_B\) (overdamped): **cannot overshoot**;
- \(T_C\) (critically damped): **cannot overshoot**.

(The same conclusion follows from the step-response residue signs, but the problem forbids computing waveforms.)

\[\boxed{A:\ \omega_n=14.0\,\mathrm{rad/s},\ \zeta=0.200,\ \mathrm{underdamped},\ p=-2.80\pm j13.72,\ y_{\infty}=1,\ \mathrm{can\ OS};
\ B:\ \omega_n=4\sqrt{3}=6.928\,\mathrm{rad/s},\ \zeta=2/\sqrt{3}=1.155,\ \mathrm{overdamped},\ p=-4,-12,\ y_{\infty}=1,\ \mathrm{no\ OS};
\ C:\ \omega_n=5.00\,\mathrm{rad/s},\ \zeta=1,\ \mathrm{critical},\ p=-5,-5,\ y_{\infty}=1,\ \mathrm{no\ OS}}\]

