**Assumption check.** Two real poles, no zeros; \(K\) of either sign. The damping coefficient of the closed-loop quadratic is independent of \(K\), so there is no \(j\omega\) crossing at finite \(K\).

**(a)** \((s+3)(s+7)+K=s^2+10s+(21+K)\). Routh–Hurwitz: \(10>0\) always, and \(21+K>0\), i.e. \(K>-21\). Open interval: \(K>-21\).

**(b)** Poles at \(-3\) and \(-7\). For \(K\ge 0\) (odd-count): the only on-locus real segment is \([-7,-3]\). For \(K\le 0\) (even-count): \((-\infty,-7]\), \([-3,+\infty)\). (In particular the complementary interval \((-7,-3)\) is off the \(0^\circ\) locus.)

**(c)** \(K=-(s+3)(s+7)=-(s^2+10s+21)\), so \(\mathrm{d}K/\mathrm{d}s=-(2s+10)=0\) gives \(s=-5\), which lies in \([-7,-3]\). Then \(K=-(-5+3)(-5+7)=4\). This is a breakaway: the two real branches meet at \(-5\) when \(K=4\) and leave the real axis. For \(K\le 0\), as \(K\) decreases from \(0\), the branch that starts at \(-3\) moves to the **right** along \([-3,+\infty)\), and the branch that starts at \(-7\) moves to the **left** along \((-\infty,-7]\). Both remain on the real axis for all \(K\le 0\).

**(d)** A root at the origin: \(\Delta(0)=21+K=0\), so \(K=-21\). A pair \(\pm\mathrm{j}\omega\) with \(\omega>0\) would require the linear coefficient to vanish, but that coefficient is \(10\neq 0\); there is **no** finite \(K\) producing a nonzero imaginary-axis pair. This matches (a): the only finite stability boundary is \(K=-21\), a simple root at the origin (the other root is \(-10\)), and for \(K>-21\) both roots are open-LHP.

**Stability check.** At the \(K\ge 0\) breakaway, \(\Delta(s)=s^2+10s+25=(s+5)^2\), confirming a repeated real root at \(s=-5\), \(K=4\).

\[\boxed{(a)\ s^2+10s+(21+K),\ K>-21;
(b)\ K\ge 0:\ [-7,-3];\ K\le 0:\ (-\infty,-7]\cup[-3,+\infty);
(c)\ s=-5,\ K=4\ \mathrm{breakaway};\ K<0:\ -3\mathrm{\ moves\ right},\ -7\mathrm{\ moves\ left};
(d)\ K=-21\ \mathrm{at\ origin};\ \mathrm{no\ }\pm\mathrm{j}\omega;\ \mathrm{matches}\ K>-21}\]

