(c) A dense frequency scan of \(|L|-1\) finds a **unique** gain crossover at \(\omega_{\mathrm{gc}}=2.000\,\mathrm{rad/s}\), with \(\arg L=-142.9^\circ\) and \(\mathrm{PM}=37.12^\circ\). (The DC flexible factor \(\omega_z^2/\omega_p^2=9/16\) reduces low-frequency gain relative to \(L_{\mathrm{rb}}\), moving the crossover down and *increasing* PM.) The flexible resonance peaks at \(|L(\mathrm{j}\omega_p)|=0.155<1\), so it does not create a second unit-circle crossing. Continuous Bode phase of \(L\) ranges in about \((-180^\circ,-32^\circ)\) and never attains \(-180^\circ\) at finite \(\omega\) (high-frequency relative degree \(2\)). Thus there is **no** finite phase crossover, \(\mathrm{GM}=+\infty\). The flexible pair does **not** create an additional gain or phase crossover that \(L_{\mathrm{rb}}\) lacks (\(L_{\mathrm{rb}}\) likewise has a unique GC and infinite GM).

