CTRL:9927270.canonicalSolution:
(a)
\[
G(0)=\begin{pmatrix}1.80&1.90\\1.70&2.083\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\det G(0)=0.520,\qquad
\Lambda(G(0))=\begin{pmatrix}7.21&-6.21\\-6.21&7.21\end{pmatrix}.
\]
Thus \(\lambda_{11}(0)=7.21>0\) (off-diagonal \(\lambda_{12}=-6.21<0\)). The pairing rule selects the **diagonal** pairing because \(\lambda_{11}\) is positive (the off-diagonal is negative; a negative relative gain is the worse pairing). Row dominance at DC: \(|G_{11}|=1.80<1.90=|G_{12}|\), so row 1 fails; \(|G_{22}|=2.08>1.70=|G_{21}|\), so row 2 holds. \(G(0)\) is **not** row-diagonally dominant.

(b)
\[
G(\mathrm{j}2)=\begin{pmatrix}
0.360-0.720\,\mathrm{j}&0.950-0.950\,\mathrm{j}\\
0.478-0.764\,\mathrm{j}&0.551-0.919\,\mathrm{j}
\end{pmatrix}.
\]
Row 1: diagonal modulus \(0.805\), off-diagonal (Gershgorin radius) \(1.34\). Not dominant; the disk is centered at \(0.360-0.720\,\mathrm{j}\) with radius \(1.34>0.805\), so it **contains the origin**. Row 2: diagonal modulus \(1.07\), radius \(0.901\). Dominant; the disk excludes the origin. Overall, \(G(\mathrm{j}2)\) is **not** row-diagonally dominant. The first row disk contains the origin.

(c) \(\lambda_{11}(\mathrm{j}2)=-0.999+1.45\,\mathrm{j}\) (modulus \(1.76\), real part negative). The RGA at this frequency does **not** still recommend the DC (diagonal) pairing: \(\mathrm{Re}\,\lambda_{11}<0\). Dominance failure and the RGA’s left-half \(\lambda_{11}\) **agree** that diagonal pairing is unsafe at \(\omega=2.00\,\mathrm{rad/s}\).

(d) Column 1: \(|G_{11}|=0.805<0.901=|G_{21}|\), fails. Column 2: \(|G_{22}|=1.07<1.34=|G_{12}|\), fails. \(G(\mathrm{j}2)\) is **not** column-diagonally dominant. Row and column tests **agree** (both fail); the row test failed on row 1 only, while both columns fail.

(e)
\[
G_{\mathrm{d}}(\mathrm{j}2)=G(\mathrm{j}2)G(0)^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}
-1.66+0.221\,\mathrm{j}&1.97-0.658\,\mathrm{j}\\
0.110-0.0563\,\mathrm{j}&0.164-0.390\,\mathrm{j}
\end{pmatrix},
\]
with moduli \(1.68\), \(2.08\), \(0.124\), \(0.423\). Row 1: \(1.68<2.08\), not dominant. Row 2: \(0.423>0.124\), dominant. \(G_{\mathrm{d}}(\mathrm{j}2)\) is **not** row-diagonally dominant: DC decoupling did **not** restore dominance at this nonzero frequency.

Limitations of evidence. Designer 1 (discards diagonal control from a dominance failure at \(\omega=2\)): a single-frequency dominance failure does not speak to the DC RGA pairing, nor to whether a (possibly retuned) diagonal controller could still work at other frequencies or in closed loop. Designer 2 (keeps diagonal pairing from \(\lambda_{11}(0)>0\)): DC RGA does not speak to high-frequency dominance, nor to the RGA at \(\omega=2\) (where \(\mathrm{Re}\,\lambda_{11}<0\)).

\[
\boxed{(a)\ G(0)=\bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}1.80&1.90\\1.70&2.08\end{smallmatrix}\bigr),\ \lambda_{11}(0)=7.21;\ \text{diagonal pairing};\ \text{not row-diagonally dominant}
}
\]
\[
\boxed{(b)\ G(\mathrm{j}2)=\bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}0.360-0.720\mathrm{j}&0.950-0.950\mathrm{j}\\0.478-0.764\mathrm{j}&0.551-0.919\mathrm{j}\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)
}
\]
\[
\boxed{\text{row 1: }0.805<1.34\text{ (origin in disk)};\ \text{row 2: }1.07>0.901;\ \text{not row-DD}
}
\]
\[
\boxed{(c)\ \lambda_{11}(\mathrm{j}2)=-0.999+1.45\mathrm{j};\ \text{does not recommend diagonal};\ \text{dominance and RGA agree (unsafe)}
}
\]
\[
\boxed{(d)\ \text{not column-DD (both columns fail)};\ \text{row and column tests agree}
}
\]
\[
\boxed{(e)\ G_{\mathrm{d}}(\mathrm{j}2)\text{ not row-DD (row 1 fails)};\ \text{DC decoupling did not restore dominance}
}
\]
\[
\boxed{\text{DC RGA }\nRightarrow\text{ HF dominance/RGA(j2)};\ \text{one-frequency dominance failure }\nRightarrow\text{ DC pairing or other frequencies}
}
\]

